      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Practice in Banking and Financial Institutions The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Practice in Banking and Financial Institutions Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor of Practice in Banking and Financial Institutions Category: Business Administration Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Economics and Finance Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Aug 19, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 203907 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. The Woody L. Hunt College of Business is AACSB-accredited. The Department of Economics and Finance serves more than 900 students across four degree programs - BBA in Economics, BBA in Finance, MS in Economics with Data Analytics, and a PhD with Finance concentration - and houses the industry-facing Banking Academy and the Prudential Risk Management Academy. Job Description: The Department of Economics and Finance at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for a renewable, non-tenure-track faculty position in Banking and Financial Institutions, beginning January 16, 2027. The position will be filled at the rank of Assistant Professor of Practice or Assistant Professor of Instruction, depending on the qualifications of the successful candidate. We are looking for a colleague whose primary commitment is to excellent classroom teaching in banking and financial institutions. Two profiles are welcome: experienced banking professionals interested in sharing their practical knowledge in a university classroom, who may come from lending, credit, risk management, bank examination, treasury, or capital markets; and finance PhDs whose training and teaching are concentrated in banking and financial institutions. The Department's Banking and Financial Institutions concentration and its Banking Academy prepare students for careers in banking and other financial institutions of this region and beyond, and this position is central to both. Teach undergraduate banking courses, including Bank Management, Bank Lending, Central Banking, and Current Issues in Banking, along with other banking courses to be developed and other undergraduate finance courses as needed. Depending on qualifications, teaching may include master's-level courses. Contribute to the Banking Academy and to the Department's relationships with regional financial institutions, including guest speakers, student mentoring, internship pipelines, and career placement. Attain and maintain the AACSB faculty qualification appropriate to the appointment ? Practice Academic (PA) or Instructional Practitioner (IP) for the Professor of Practice rank, and Scholarly Academic (SA) or Practice Academic (PA) for the Professor of Instruction rank ? through continued professional engagement, applied or pedagogical scholarship, or industry-relevant contributions. Serve on departmental and college committees and participate in community outreach. Requirements: Candidates need satisfy the requirements of only one track. Applicants should indicate in their letter of interest which track they are applying under; the search committee may also consider an applicant under the other track where the record warrants. Assistant Professor of Practice track A master's degree in finance, economics, business, or a closely related field, plus at least five years of substantive professional experience in banking or financial institutions. Assistant Professor of Instruction track A PhD in Finance with a concentration in Banking and Financial Institutions, or a PhD in a closely related field with a demonstrated specialization in banking. University-level teaching experience in banking and financial institutions courses. Both tracks Demonstrated capacity for effective classroom instruction. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, and the ability to work productively with students from a wide range of backgrounds. Preferred Qualifications: Documented evidence of teaching effectiveness, including student evaluations and peer observations. Demonstrated competence in the instructional use of artificial intelligence tools, including their use in course and assessment design and in preparing students to apply AI appropriately to banking and financial analysis. Senior-level banking experience and an active professional network in the industry; for candidates on the Instruction track, prior professional experience in banking or financial institutions. Relevant professional credentials (e.g., CFA, CFP, FRM). Familiarity with the U.S.-Mexico border economy or binational banking. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applicants are encouraged to apply by October 15, 2026. Applicants must submit the following through the UTEP employment system: a letter of interest addressing your fit with the position; a current curriculum vitae; a statement of teaching philosophy and, where available, evidence of teaching effectiveness; and complete contact information for three professional references. Graduate transcripts will be requested in the later stages of the search. Inquiries about the position may be directed to Zuobao Wei at zwei@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204112": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Mental Health Counseling",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor of Mental Health Counseling The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor of Mental Health Counseling Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant/Associate Professor of Mental Health Counseling Category: Education Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Counseling and Special Education Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Aug 25, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-CoEd-01 Position ID: 204112 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. The College of Education consists of three departments - Counseling and Special Education, Educational Leadership and Foundations, and Teacher Education, which has two divisions: Bilingual Education, Early Childhood Education, Literacy/Biliteracy, Sociocultural Studies (BELSS), and Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics (STEM) Education. These departments employ more than 60 tenured and tenure-track faculty members and enroll more than 1,800 undergraduates and 1,200 graduate students. The Department of Counseling and Special Education offers programs at the master's level in special education, educational diagnostics, clinical mental health, rehabilitation, and school counseling. The department also provides the content courses for the generic special education specialization in the Bachelor of Independent Studies (BIS) degree program. In addition, the department provides three \"service\" courses (EDPC 2300, EDPC 3300 and SPED 3310) to all education majors. The department offers a core course (EDPC 1301) that focuses on career development for incoming students. Job Description: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant/Associate Professor for our CACREP accredited Counseling Programs beginning in Fall 2027, housed in the Department of Counseling and Special Education in the College of Education. The successful candidates will be committed to excellence in teaching, research, service, and community engagement as well as collaboration across the university. Teaching assignments will be based on candidates' experience and expertise. The successful candidates will assume a range of responsibilities including the following: (1) teaching graduate level courses, including in-person, hybrid, and online courses; (2) developing a sustained program of research and scholarship; (3) participating in efforts to seek external funding; (4) participating in broad-based university, college, and departmental committee work, as well as professional and community service; (5) collaboration with colleagues, (6) advising students; and (7) supervising students' field experiences. Teaching in summer is generally available. Requirements: An earned doctorate in Counselor Education from a CACREP approved program or closely related field [ABD considered but doctorate must be completed before employment] Evidence of teaching effectiveness in graduate-level courses. Evidence of a sustainable program of scholarship and research. Relevant experience in the field of Mental Health Counseling Evidence of the ability to work collaboratively with other faculty, professionals, and community organizations. Commitment to maintenance of CACREP standards. Preferred Qualifications Bilingual in English and Spanish Experience as a clinical mental health counselor An understanding of and commitment to community and globally engaged scholarship Certification as a Licensed Professional Counselor Licensed as a Professional Counselor in Texas or eligible to be Licensed as a Professional Counselor in Texas Ability and flexibility to teach both foundational and advanced Counseling courses Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. The successful applicant will commence duties Fall 2027. Applicants must submit the following information: Curriculum vita; Letter of application that addresses the required and preferred qualifications; A sample of research publications; A statement of contributions to inclusive excellence; and Names and contact information of three references (references will not be contacted without the applicant's approval and not until he/she has been selected for an on campus visit). For questions about the position, you may contact: Dr. Johana Rocha, Search Committee Chair: jrocha10@utep.edu or (915) 747-7584. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205291": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Aerospace Engineering (2 Positions)",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor in Aerospace Engineering (2 Positions) The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor in Aerospace Engineering (2 Positions) Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor in Aerospace Engineering (2 Positions) Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 28, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-LCEng-02/04 Position ID: 205291 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. Job Description: The Department of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for two Aerospace Engineering faculty positions beginning in Fall 2027. The successful candidates will establish or expand nationally recognized, externally funded research programs; teach effectively at the undergraduate and graduate levels; mentor students; contribute to departmental and university service; and collaborate across disciplinary boundaries. Candidates in the listed primary emphasis areas are particularly encouraged to apply but all areas of aerospace engineering will be considered. The appointments are intended for emerging scholars with compelling research visions and strong potential for externally funded scholarship. Relevant departmental strengths and opportunities include autonomous aerospace systems, orbital mechanics and astrodynamics, spacecraft dynamics and control, close-proximity operations, space situational awareness, aerospace materials, propulsion, hypersonics, and space systems design. The Department of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering offers B.S. programs in Aerospace Engineering and Mechanical Engineering, M.S. programs in Aerospace Engineering and Mechanical Engineering, and a Ph.D. program in Mechanical Engineering, as well as a graduate certificate in 3D Engineering and Additive Manufacturing. In Fall 2025, the department enrolled 1,735 students, including 1,590 undergraduates, 75 master's students, and 70 doctoral students. The department has an active, collaborative research culture, and strong engagement of graduate students. Faculty have access to extensive research infrastructure and interdisciplinary partnerships, including the Aerospace Center, the W.M. Keck Center for 3D Innovation, and the Advanced Manufacturing and Aerospace Center. The new approximately 93,000-square-foot, $80 million Advanced Manufacturing and Aerospace Center expands laboratories, teaching spaces, and collaboration opportunities in advanced manufacturing, aerospace systems, materials, autonomy, and related fields. Requirements: An earned doctorate in Aerospace Engineering or a closely related field by the anticipated start date. Evidence of research achievement and strong potential to develop an externally funded research program. Potential for excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching and student mentoring. Commitment to interdisciplinary collaboration and service to the department, university, and profession. Preferred Qualifications: All areas of aerospace engineering; particular interest in aerospace materials, autonomy, astrodynamics, close-proximity operations, and space situational awareness Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants should submit: (1) a cover letter describing their interest in the position and alignment with UTEP's mission; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a statement of research accomplishments and future plans; (4) a statement of teaching philosophy and interests; and (5) names and contact information for at least three professional references. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the positions are filled. Questions about these searches may be directed to Angel Flores-Abad, Ph.D., Search Committee Chair, at afloresabad@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205028": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Computer Science",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor in Computer Science The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor in Computer Science Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor in Computer Science Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Computer Science Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 21, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-LCEng-07 Position ID: 205028 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. Job Description: UTEP's CS department is part of the Miguel A. Loya College of Engineering, along with six other academic departments. The CS department is deeply committed to student professional development and advancement through faculty mentoring and research engagement at all levels. Leading companies and government agencies heavily recruit our graduates. The college offers students interdisciplinary and industry training opportunities, study abroad programs, and other professional development opportunities. The college is consistently ranked among the top schools in the U.S. in awarding bachelor's degrees to Hispanics. The department offers B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. degrees in Computer Science, a B.S. in Artificial Intelligence, and M.S. degrees in Software Engineering and Artificial Intelligence. The B.S. in CS offers concentrations in Software Engineering, Secure Cyber-Systems, and Data Analytics, with a Secure Cyber-Systems concentration also available at the graduate level. Through CS undergraduate and graduate programs, UTEP is designated as an NSA Center of Academic Excellence in Cyber Defense, Cyber Operations, and Cybersecurity Research. The department values interdisciplinary research and does research in collaboration with numerous external and internal partners. CS faculty lead impactful research in areas such as Software Engineering, Computer Systems, Cybersecurity, Artificial Intelligence, and Theoretical Foundations of CS.  Our faculty are nationally recognized for their scholarship and national leadership in professional organizations, and the department consistently ranks among the highest at UTEP in research expenditures. With a vibrant academic community of 25 tenured and tenure-track faculty members, as well as four instructional faculty members, we foster innovation, collaboration, and student success. The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor Position in Computer Science (CS), with a research focus on Software Engineering, including but not limited to Software Development and Maintenance, Software Testing and Verification, Software Architecture and Design, Programming Languages and Software Tools, Software Analytics and Mining, Empirical Software Engineering, and AI for Software Engineering, starting in the Fall of 2027. Candidates are expected to have a record of high-quality scholarship and should be able to demonstrate the potential for excellence in both research and teaching. The department values and encourages interdisciplinary research, providing a unique opportunity for collaboration across fields. We also value industry and government experience, recognizing the importance of real-world applications in research, in particular for this position. Requirements: Applicants must hold a doctorate in CS or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Successful candidates must demonstrate excellent communication skills, the ability to establish an active research program, and a strong commitment to student success and to UTEP's Access to Excellent Higher Education mission. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Applications must include 1) a cover letter (2 pages maximum), 2) a curriculum vitae, 3) a research statement (3 pages maximum), 4) a teaching statement (2 pages maximum), and 5) contact information for at least three references. For additional information about the position, contact Dr. Yoonsik Cheon, Search Committee Chair, via email at ycheon@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205031": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Computer Science",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Computer Science The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Computer Science Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor of Computer Science Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Computer Science Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 21, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-LCEng-06 Position ID: 205031 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. Job Description: UTEP's CS department is part of the Miguel A. Loya College of Engineering, along with six other academic departments. The CS department is deeply committed to student professional development and advancement through faculty mentoring and research engagement at all levels. Leading companies and government agencies heavily recruit our graduates. The college offers students interdisciplinary and industry training opportunities, study abroad programs, and other professional development opportunities. The college is consistently ranked among the top schools in the U.S. in awarding bachelor's degrees to Hispanics. The department offers B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. degrees in Computer Science, a B.S. in Artificial Intelligence, and M.S. degrees in Software Engineering and Artificial Intelligence. The B.S. in CS offers concentrations in Software Engineering, Secure Cyber-Systems, and Data Analytics, with a Secure Cyber-Systems concentration also available at the graduate level. Through CS undergraduate and graduate programs, UTEP is designated as an NSA Center of Academic Excellence in Cyber Defense, Cyber Operations, and Cybersecurity Research. The department values interdisciplinary research and does research in collaboration with numerous external and internal partners. CS faculty lead impactful research in areas such as Software Engineering, Computer Systems, Cybersecurity, Artificial Intelligence, and Theoretical Foundations of CS. Our faculty are nationally recognized for their scholarship and national leadership in professional organizations, and the department consistently ranks among the highest at UTEP in research expenditures. With a vibrant academic community of 25 tenured and tenure-track faculty members, as well as four instructional faculty members, we foster innovation, collaboration, and student success. The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor Position in Computer Science (CS), with a research focus on Systems, including but not limited to Operating Systems, Distributed Systems, Embedded Systems, HPC, Compilers, Software Design and Validation for Real-time and Numerical Systems, Programming Languages, Signal Processing and System Control, Systems Security and Reliability, and Computer Networks, starting in the Fall of 2027. Candidates are expected to have a record of high-quality scholarship and should be able to demonstrate the potential for excellence in both research and teaching. The department values and encourages interdisciplinary research, providing a unique opportunity for collaboration across fields. We also value industry experience, recognizing the importance of real-world applications in research, in particular for this position. Requirements: Applicants must hold a doctorate in CS or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Successful candidates must demonstrate excellent communication skills, the ability to establish an active research program, and a strong commitment to student success and to UTEP's Access to Excellent Higher Education mission. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin on November 30, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Applications must include 1) a cover letter (2 pages maximum), 2) a curriculum vitae, 3) a research statement (3 pages maximum), 4) a teaching statement (2 pages maximum), and 5) contact information for at least three references. For additional information about the position, contact Dr. Christoph Lauter, Search Committee Chair, via email at cqlauter@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204629": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Electrical and Computer Engineering",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Electrical and Computer Engineering The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Electrical and Computer Engineering Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor of Electrical and Computer Engineering Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Electrical and Computer Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 9, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-LCEng-08 Position ID: 204629 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. Job Description: The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor faculty position. The department seeks candidates with expertise in robotics, autonomous systems, and control. Applicants with expertise in related areas of electrical and computer engineering are also encouraged to apply. The department is interested in candidates whose research complements existing faculty expertise and contributes to departmental research priorities through fundamental or applied research. Evaluation will be based on the quality, originality, and potential impact of the candidate's work, as well as their potential to contribute to the department and university. The successful candidate is expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses, supervise student research, contribute to departmental, university, and professional service activities, and maintain an externally funded research program. Application materials should demonstrate the candidate's potential to meet these expectations. ABOUT THE DEPARTMENT: The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) at UTEP (https://ece.utep.edu) offers B.S. and M.S. degrees in Electrical Engineering and Computer Engineering, and a Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering. The department has 25 faculty members, enrolls more than 900 students, and supports over $5.5 million in annual sponsored research expenditures (FY 2025). ECE faculty conduct research in laboratories and research centers across the department, college, and university. Relevant facilities at the department include the Robotics and Autonomous Systems Laboratory equipped with the Quanser Drone Research Laboratory and Self-Driving Car Studio that supports research in autonomous aerial and ground systems, controls, navigation, computer vision, sensor fusion, machine learning, and cyber-physical systems. The Cyber Physical Systems Privacy and Security Enhanced Computing (CyPSEC) Laboratory hosts an OPAL-RT Real-Time Simulation Laboratory that provides real-time simulation and hardware-in-the-loop capabilities for control and cyber-physical systems research. The department also maintains a Wireless Communications Testbed that supports research in wireless communications, software-defined radio, wireless security, spectrum characterization, telecommunications systems, and 4G/5G networks. Requirements: A Ph.D. (or equivalent degree) in Electrical and/or Computer Engineering or a related field before the official position start date. Commitment to undergraduate and graduate education. Demonstrated scholarly achievements as evidenced by a record of publications. Demonstrated potential for establishing an extramurally funded research program. Excellent communication skills. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants must submit the following: 1) a letter of interest describing their interest in the position; 2) detailed curriculum vitae; 3) name and complete contact information of at least three references; 4) research statement (up to 3 pages); 5) teaching and mentoring statement (up to 3 pages); 6) unofficial transcripts; and 7) copies of the candidate's two most representative publications. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. For information/inquiries regarding the position, please email, ECE_Faculty_Search@utep.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=195300": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Instruction in Mining Engineering",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Instruction in Mining Engineering The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Instruction in Mining Engineering Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor of Instruction in Mining Engineering Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Metallurgical, Materials and Biomedical Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Dec 3, 2025 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 195300 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1 and community engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls 25,000 students - 85% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 171 bachelor's, master's, and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. METALLURGICAL, MATERIALS & BIOMEDICAL ENGINEERING DEPARTMENT The MMBME Department ( https://www.utep.edu/engineering/mmbme/ ) currently offers undergraduate degree in Metallurgical and Materials Engineering, M.S.  degrees in Metallurgical and Materials Engineering and Biomedical Engineering as well as Ph.D. in Materials Science and Engineering and Biomedical Engineering. Beginning Fall 2027, the department will admit the first cohort of students for the B.S. in Mining Engineering. MMBME faculty are actively engaged in sponsored research with more than $2.4 million in annual expenditures. Faculty collaborate with each other and with faculty in the Colleges of Science and Engineering. Research activities are conducted in multiple departmental laboratories and across research centers. Job Description: The Department of Metallurgical, Materials, and Biomedical Engineering (MMBME) at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for a full-time, non-tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor of Instruction with an appointment as the Mining Engineering Undergraduate Program Director. Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. MINING ENGINEERING PROGRAM RESTART With the growth and increasing national importance of the mining economy, the University of Texas System Board of Regents has committed $20 million to restart the Mining Engineering program at UTEP. The Texas State Legislature committed another $20 million to fund the infrastructure needed to teach Mining Engineering. The program builds on UTEP's rich mining legacy and regional heritage. Founded in 1914 as the State School of Mines and Metallurgy, the university joined the UT System in 1919 as the Texas College of Mines and Metallurgy and continued to award the Mining Engineering Degree until 1966. Texas now has the largest non-fuel mineral production economy in the United States (USGS Mineral Commodity Summary 2024), and UTEP will offer the only Mining Engineering undergraduate program in Texas. The relaunch of the Mining Engineering program comes with dedicated support from industry partners committed to establishing a world-class modern mining engineering program. POSITION OVERVIEW Candidates for Assistant Professor of Instruction who will also serve as the Undergraduate Program Director in the planned Mining Engineering program can be from all areas of mining engineering, including but not limited to: Mine Planning & Design, Geomechanics, Mineral Processing, Rock Fragmentation & Blasting, Mining Ventilation, Mine Automation, Health & Safety, or closely related fields. This is a unique opportunity for a dedicated educator and academic leader to help rebuild, shape, and champion a modern mining engineering program. The successful candidate will play a central role in curriculum development, program launch, student success, industry engagement, and community outreach. Pre-Launch Responsibilities (Before Program Start in Fall 2027): Prior to welcoming the first cohort of students, the successful candidate will contribute to the foundational work necessary to reestablish a forward-looking mining engineering program. This includes coordinating a comprehensive curricular redesign such as developing new course proposals, updating syllabi, integrating modern technologies, and aligning the curriculum with industry needs and ABET expectations. The candidate will provide administrative leadership as Undergraduate Program Director by planning course schedules, contributing to academic policies, preparing catalog updates and submissions, and coordinating extensively with the College of Engineering. Additional responsibilities include supporting strategic recruitment efforts, developing promotional materials, engaging community colleges, and representing the program at regional and national events as needed. This faculty member will also organize major outreach initiatives such as K-12 partnerships, high-school and teacher summer camps, STEM events, Mine Day activities, and new-student orientation programs. Collaboration with the Industry Advisory Board will be essential to ensure that industry needs and trends inform program development. The role also involves contributing to laboratory planning and modernization by identifying equipment needs, developing lab syllabi, and supporting future facility upgrades to be aligned with the planned curriculum. Post-Launch Responsibilities (After First Cohort Admission in Fall 2027): Once the undergraduate program launches, the role will transition into a combined instructional and program leadership position. The undergraduate program director will teach undergraduate mining engineering courses using student-centered, experiential, and innovative instructional approaches and will mentor Senior Design (Capstone) projects to ensure meaningful industry-engaged project experiences. The candidate will support and coordinate ABET assessment and evaluation activities, including outcomes assessment, continuous improvement processes, and accreditation reports preparation. They will oversee the student career mentoring program by building employer connections, coordinating internships, and guiding students toward strong career placements. Additional responsibilities include advising student clubs such as SME and Women in Mining, fostering leadership and professional development, and maintaining active outreach and community engagement to strengthen K-12 and regional pipelines. The faculty member will also cultivate strong industry partnerships through guest lectures, mine tours, webinars, scholarship initiatives, and professional networking events. Requirements: The ideal candidate must hold a Ph.D. in Mining Engineering or related fields and demonstrate excellence or strong potential in engineering education, proven leadership and organizational skills, and a deep commitment to student success. The candidate should be collaborative, able to work effectively with faculty, industry partners, and diverse student populations, and passionate about building and advancing academic programs through curriculum innovation and community engagement. An understanding of contemporary issues in mining, such as sustainability, digital technologies, automation, safety, and workforce trends, is a plus. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. For full consideration, applicants must submit: Letter of interest and leadership experience Detailed curriculum vitae Names and contact information of at least three references Statement of teaching interests Outreach vision statement Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=195798": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Mining Engineering (2 Positions)",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor in Mining Engineering (2 Positions) The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor in Mining Engineering (2 Positions) Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant/Associate Professor in Mining Engineering (2 Positions) Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Metallurgical, Materials and Biomedical Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Dec 17, 2025 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 26-COEN-12,13 Position ID: 195798 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1 and community engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls 25,000 students - 85% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 171 bachelor's, master's, and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. METALLURGICAL, MATERIALS & BIOMEDICAL ENG. DEPARTMENT The MMBME Department ( https://www.utep.edu/engineering/mmbme/ ) currently offers an undergraduate degree in Metallurgical and Materials Engineering, M.S. degrees in both Metallurgical and Materials Engineering and Biomedical Engineering as well as Ph.D. degrees in Materials Science and Engineering and Biomedical Engineering. Beginning in Fall 2027, the department will admit its first cohort of students pursuing a B.S. in Mining Engineering. MMBME faculty are actively engaged in sponsored research with more than $2.4 million in annual expenditures. Faculty collaborate with each other and with faculty in the Colleges of Science and Engineering. Research activities are conducted in multiple departmental laboratories and across research centers. Job Description: The Department of Metallurgical, Materials, and Biomedical Engineering (MMBME) at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for two full-time, tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor to support the relaunch of the Mining Engineering program. Competitive salaries will be offered and will be commensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank. MINING ENGINEERING PROGRAM RESTART To address the national shortage of mining engineering professionals amid the growing strategic importance of domestic mineral production and supply-chain security, the University of Texas System Board of Regents has committed $20 million to restart the Mining Engineering program at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP). In addition, the Texas State Legislature has allocated $20 million to support the infrastructure and facilities required for high-quality mining engineering education, supplemented by multiple significant contributions from industry partners. The relaunched program builds upon UTEP's deep mining heritage and regional legacy. Founded in 1914 as the State School of Mines and Metallurgy, the institution joined the University of Texas System in 1919 as the Texas College of Mines and Metallurgy and awarded degrees in Mining Engineering until 1966. Today, Texas leads the nation in non-fuel mineral production (USGS Mineral Commodity Summary, 2024), and UTEP will offer the only undergraduate Mining Engineering program in the state. The program is being reestablished with strong and sustained industry engagement, positioning it to become a world-class, modern mining engineering program that serves both regional and national workforce needs. POSITIONS OVERVIEW Candidates for these Mining Engineering faculty positions may come from any area of mining engineering, including but not limited to Mine Planning and Design, Geomechanics, Mineral Processing and Extractive Metallurgy, Rock Fragmentation and Blasting, Mining Ventilation, Mine Automation, Health and Safety, Digital Mining Systems, and closely related fields. The department particularly encourages applications from candidates whose expertise emphasizes emerging technologies, data-driven approaches, sustainability, and modern mining systems. Demonstrated research and scholarly publication experience, along with meaningful industry experience, are highly valued, as they will strengthen the program's research profile, instructional relevance, and industry alignment. These positions present unique opportunities to develop a contemporary mining engineering program with state-of-the-art instructional and research laboratories, backed by strong industry partnerships and focused on workforce development, innovation, and societal impact. The key responsibilities and/or capabilities of these faculty members are: 1.1 Teaching Develop and teach undergraduate and graduate courses in Mining Engineering, aligned with the candidate's area of expertise and department needs. Integrate emerging and modern technologies into instruction, including automation, data analytics, artificial intelligence, and advanced modeling and simulation tools. Mentor and advise undergraduate and graduate students, including supervision of undergraduate research and design projects, master's theses, and doctoral dissertations. Contribute to curriculum development and modernization, including incorporation of best practices in safety, sustainability, and innovation, and support accreditation and continuous improvement efforts. 1.2 Research Establish and sustain a strong, externally funded research program that addresses critical challenges in mining and mineral resources engineering, with an emphasis on emerging technologies and intelligent, data-driven approaches. Secure competitive research funding and disseminate research outcomes through high-impact, peer-reviewed journals, conference presentations, graduate student training and completion, and meaningful industry engagement. Preferred research areas include, but are not limited to: Geomechanics and Advanced Rock Mechanics: Rock characterization, numerical modeling, ground control, and geotechnical risk management for deep underground and surface mining operations. Advanced Mineral Processing and Extractive Technologies: Comminution, separation processes, hydro/pyrometallurgy, and advanced recovery of critical minerals from primary or non-conventional resources. Smart and Digital Mining Systems: Digital twins, mine automation, robotics, autonomous equipment, and advanced monitoring, sensing, and control systems, including image processing, particle analysis, machine learning, and big-data analytics for mine and plant optimization. Sustainability, Safety, and Mining Environment: Mine safety and health technologies, advanced ventilation systems, emissions control, environmental monitoring, and exposure health studies. 1.3 Service Serve on departmental, college, and university committees, contributing to shared governance and academic leadership. Support departmental and institutional strategic initiatives, including program development, ABET accreditation, student recruitment, and outreach activities. Engage actively with industry partners and advisory boards to strengthen research relevance, enhance opportunities for students, and align educational outcomes with industry needs. Participate in professional societies such as the Society for Mining, Metallurgy & Exploration (SME) and other relevant organizations. Contribute to the broader scholarly community through editorial service, peer review, conference organization, and professional leadership activities. Prior to welcoming the first cohort of students, the successful candidates will contribute to the foundational work required to reestablish a forward-looking mining engineering program. Responsibilities will include supporting the establishment of instructional and research laboratories by identifying equipment needs, developing laboratory experiments, and planning future upgrades. The role will also involve developing new courses, aligning course content with industry needs and ABET expectations, preparing research proposals, and recruiting and advising graduate students. Additional duties include supporting strategic student recruitment efforts and representing the program at professional, industry, and outreach events. Following the launch of the undergraduate program, the position will transition into a combined teaching, research, and service role, consistent with tenure-track faculty expectations. Requirements: The ideal candidate must hold a Ph.D. in Mining Engineering or a closely related field from an accredited institution. A strong record of peer-reviewed scholarly publications and demonstrated success in securing externally funded research support are highly preferred. The candidate should have proven experience in interdisciplinary collaboration and in developing productive industry partnerships. Prior teaching experience in mining engineering or closely related disciplines is highly desirable and professional experience in the mining industry and Professional Engineer (PE) licensure is strongly valued as an asset. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. For full consideration, applicants must submit: Cover letter Detailed curriculum vitae Names and contact information of at least three references Statement of teaching philosophy Statement of research interests Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204627": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Metallurgical, Materials and Biomedical Engineering",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor of Metallurgical, Materials and Biomedical Engineering The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor of Metallurgical, Materials and Biomedical Engineering Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant/Associate Professor of Metallurgical, Materials and Biomedical Engineering Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Metallurgical, Materials and Biomedical Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 9, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-LCEng-10 Position ID: 204627 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. Job Description: The Department of Metallurgical, Materials and Biomedical Engineering (MMBME) at The University of Texas at El Paso is one of the university's oldest and most research-intensive engineering departments. Its origins trace back to UTEP's founding as the Texas State School of Mines and Metallurgy in 1914, reflecting a long-standing tradition of excellence in minerals, materials, and engineering education. MMBME is part of the Miguel A. Loya College of Engineering and is dedicated to advancing knowledge in the processing, structure, properties, and performance of materials, while also addressing challenges in healthcare through biomedical engineering. The department combines fundamental scientific principles with engineering innovation to develop solutions for energy, manufacturing, transportation, infrastructure, medicine, and national security. The department offers undergraduate and graduate programs that prepare students for careers in industry, research, healthcare technology, and academia, including: B.S. in Metallurgical and Materials Engineering B.S. in Mining Engineering (starting Fall 2027) M.S. in Metallurgical and Materials Engineering Ph.D. in Materials Science and Engineering M.S. in Biomedical Engineering Ph.D. in Biomedical Engineering Minor in Materials Science and Engineering Minor in Biomedical Engineering MMBME is recognized for its strong emphasis on experiential learning, undergraduate research, laboratory education, and industry engagement. Students gain hands-on experience through state-of-the-art facilities and collaborative research projects involving advanced materials characterization, additive manufacturing, biomaterials, tissue engineering, extractive metallurgy, aerospace materials, sensors, polymers, materials for extreme environments, and emerging technologies. The department maintains a robust research portfolio supported by federal agencies, national laboratories, and industrial partners. Faculty and students conduct interdisciplinary research in areas such as: Materials characterization and performance Materials in extreme environments Materials development Computational materials engineering Advanced manufacturing and additive manufacturing Critical materials and materials for energy storage and sustainability Engineering education Biomaterials, tissue engineering and nanoparticle drug delivery methods Building on its historic mining heritage, UTEP has recently relaunched its Mining Engineering program with significant support from the University of Texas System, the Texas Legislature, and industry partners, further strengthening the department's role in addressing workforce needs related to critical minerals, resource extraction, and sustainable materials development. The Department of Metallurgical, Materials and Biomedical Engineering (MMBME) at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for a tenure-track faculty position at the rank of Assistant Professor or Associate Professor in Computational Metallurgy and Materials Engineering. Exceptional candidates with an established record of scholarship, external funding, and leadership may be considered for appointment at the Associate Professor rank. The successful candidate will contribute to UTEP's growing strengths in computational materials science, advanced manufacturing, materials design, and digital engineering while supporting the department's strategic vision aligned with the Materials Genome Initiative (MGI), Integrated Computational Materials Engineering (ICME), artificial intelligence, and data-driven materials discovery. Areas of Interest The department seeks candidates with a primary expertise in computational modeling of advanced manufacturing processes and prediction of the structure of materials.  A successful candidate must possess an expertise in one or more of the following areas: Computational materials science Computational metallurgy Integrated Computational Materials Engineering (ICME) CALPHAD and computational thermodynamics Phase-field modeling Density Functional Theory (DFT) Microstructure prediction and evolution Secondary expertise in the following topics is desired but not required: Molecular dynamics Materials informatics Machine learning and artificial intelligence for materials design Advanced manufacturing and additive manufacturing Computational geometallurgy Primary Responsibilities 1. Teaching The successful candidate will: Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in metallurgical and materials engineering. Develop new courses in computational materials science, materials informatics, AI-assisted materials design, and ICME. Integrate computational tools into core courses including thermodynamics, kinetics, physical metallurgy, materials processing, corrosion, and materials selection. Mentor undergraduate researchers, M.S. students, and Ph.D. students. Contribute to curriculum development and continuous improvement efforts supporting ABET-accredited programs. Potential specialty courses may include: Computational Materials Engineering Materials Informatics and Machine Learning ICME for Materials Design Computational Thermodynamics and CALPHAD Digital Twins for Manufacturing Systems Computational Geometallurgy Advanced Alloy Design and Optimization 2. Research The selected candidate will be expected to: Establish and maintain a nationally recognized, externally funded research program. Advance computational approaches for materials design, processing, characterization, and performance prediction. Conduct interdisciplinary research integrating experiments, modeling, simulation, and data analytics. Collaborate with faculty across engineering, science, and computing disciplines. Support research initiatives in advanced manufacturing, critical materials, aerospace, energy, defense, electronics, biomedical materials, and sustainability. Publish in high-impact journals and contribute to the department's national and international reputation. Collaboration Opportunities The position offers significant opportunities to collaborate with: Materials Science and Engineering (MASE) doctoral program faculty Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering Electrical and Computer Engineering Computer Science Physics Chemistry Institute for Applied AI Innovation Federal/State Agencies and National laboratories Government and defense agencies Advanced manufacturing and materials industries Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D. in Metallurgical Engineering, Materials Science and Engineering, Computational Materials Science, or a closely related field. Demonstrated potential for excellence in teaching and research. Ability to develop an externally funded research program. Strong written and oral communication skills. Preferred Qualifications: Expertise in computational methods for metallurgical systems. Experience with CALPHAD, DFT, molecular dynamics, phase-field methods, machine learning, or related computational approaches. Experience integrating computational and experimental materials research. Evidence of interdisciplinary collaboration. Track record of external funding and scholarly publications (especially for Associate Professor candidates). Interest in applications related to critical materials, advanced manufacturing, aerospace, defense, energy, and mining. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Interested candidates must submit the following application materials: Cover Letter describing interest in the position, relevant qualifications, and alignment with the department's mission and strategic priorities. Curriculum Vitae detailing educational background, professional experience, publications, presentations, funded research, and other scholarly activities. Research Statement (up to 5 pages) outlining current research accomplishments, future research plans, opportunities for interdisciplinary collaboration, and strategies for securing external funding. Teaching Statement (up to 3 pages) describing teaching philosophy, instructional experience, proposed course development, and strategies for mentoring and supporting student success. Names and Contact Information for Three References who can address the candidate's research, teaching, and professional qualifications. Applicants seeking appointment at the Associate Professor rank should provide evidence of: An established record of scholarly achievement; Successful acquisition of externally funded research; Graduate student mentoring; National and/or international recognition in their field. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204632": {
      "title": "Full/Associate Professor in Electrical and Computer Engineering",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Full/Associate Professor in Electrical and Computer Engineering The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Full/Associate Professor in Electrical and Computer Engineering Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Full/Associate Professor in Electrical and Computer Engineering Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Electrical and Computer Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 9, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-LCEng-09 Position ID: 204632 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. Job Description: The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for a faculty position at the rank of tenured Full Professor or tenured/tenure-track Associate Professor. Areas of interest include direct digital manufacturing (DDM), including additive manufacturing and 3D printing of electrical, electronic, and electromagnetic devices and systems, as well as computational electromagnetics. Applicants with strong records in related areas of electrical and computer engineering are also encouraged to apply. Candidates should have a record of scholarship, research leadership, and external funding appropriate for the appointment at the rank sought. Outstanding candidates currently holding tenure-track Assistant Professor positions who are expected to meet the requirements for promotion to Associate Professor with tenure during the hiring process will also be considered. The successful candidate is expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses, supervise student research, contribute to departmental, university, and professional service activities, and maintain an externally funded research program. Application materials should provide evidence of the candidate's ability to meet these expectations. ABOUT THE DEPARTMENT: The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) at UTEP offers B.S. and M.S. degrees in Electrical Engineering and Computer Engineering, as well as a Ph.D. in Electrical and Computer Engineering. The department has 25 faculty members, enrolls more than 900 students and supports over $5.5 million in annual sponsored research expenditures (FY 2025). ECE faculty conduct research in laboratories and research centers across the department, college, and university. Departmental facilities include a 6,000-square-foot cleanroom with Class 100 and Class 1000 spaces supporting semiconductor fabrication and characterization, including nanoscale 3D printing, thin-film deposition, plasma etching, photolithography, microscopy, spectroscopy, and electrical characterization. Additional departmental research infrastructure includes advanced additive manufacturing facilities featuring metal, polymer, and multi-process 3D printing systems, as well as access to the UTEP Keck Center for 3D Innovation. The department also maintains strong capabilities for microwave, millimeter-wave, and antenna research, including an anechoic chamber, instrumentation supporting measurements up to 40 GHz, and industry-standard design and simulation tools such as Ansys HFSS. In addition, the Wireless Communications Testbed supports research in wireless communications, software-defined radio, wireless security, spectrum characterization, telecommunications systems, and 4G/5G networks. Requirements: MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Ph.D. in Electrical Engineering, Computer Engineering, or a closely related field. Demonstrated commitment to undergraduate and graduate education. Established record of significant scholarly, creative, entrepreneurial, or technological contributions, evidenced by publications, patents, and/or other research accomplishments. Excellent written and oral communication skills. Established record of securing extramural funding, developing and sustaining a research program, mentoring students and postdoctoral researchers, and leading collaborative research efforts. Evidence of national and international recognition through publications, invited presentations, honors, awards, and other professional achievements. This position is intended for established researchers at the senior level. Accordingly, recent Ph.D. graduates and postdoctoral fellows are not eligible for consideration. Academic Candidates: Candidates from academic institutions must have qualifications commensurate with appointment at the rank of tenured Full Professor or tenured or tenure-track Associate Professor. Application materials should provide evidence of scholarship, leadership, and research funding appropriate to the rank sought. Candidates from National Laboratories or Industry: Candidates from national laboratories or industry must have at least six years of progressively responsible research experience following completion of the Ph.D. or postdoctoral training. Applicants must demonstrate a record of scholarship, extramural funding, research and leadership, and professional achievements comparable to those expected for appointment at the rank sought. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Experience leading large, collaborative research programs; establishing partnerships with industry, national laboratories, and government agencies; translating research into practice through technology transfer, commercialization, or entrepreneurship; leading academic or professional initiatives; mentoring diverse students and researchers; and developing academic programs or research infrastructure. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants must submit the following: 1) a cover letter describing their interest in the position and how they meet position requirements; 2) a detailed curriculum vitae; 3) name and complete contact information of at least three references; 4) research statement (up to 4 pages); 5) teaching and mentorship statement (up to 4 pages). Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. For information or inquiries regarding the position, please email, ECE_Faculty_Search@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205284": {
      "title": "Open Rank Professor in Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Open Rank Professor in Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Open Rank Professor in Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Open Rank Professor in Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 28, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-LCEng-01/03 Position ID: 205284 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. Job Description: The Department of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering offers ABET-accredited B.S. programs in Aerospace Engineering and Mechanical Engineering, M.S. programs in Aerospace Engineering and Mechanical Engineering, and a Ph.D. program in Mechanical Engineering, as well as a graduate certificate in 3D Engineering and Additive Manufacturing. In Fall 2025, the department enrolled 1,735 students, including 1,590 undergraduates, 75 master's students, and 70 doctoral students. The department has an active, collaborative research culture, and strong engagement of graduate students. Faculty have access to extensive research infrastructure and interdisciplinary partnerships, including the Aerospace Center, the W.M. Keck Center for 3D Innovation, and the Advanced Manufacturing and Aerospace Center. The new approximately 93,000-square-foot, $80 million Advanced Manufacturing and Aerospace Center expands laboratories, teaching spaces, and collaboration opportunities in advanced manufacturing, aerospace systems, materials, autonomy, and related fields. The Department of Aerospace and Mechanical Engineering at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for two Mechanical Engineering faculty positions beginning in Fall 2027. The successful candidates will establish or expand nationally recognized, externally funded research programs; teach effectively at the undergraduate and graduate levels; mentor students; contribute to departmental and university service; and collaborate across disciplinary boundaries. Candidates in the listed primary emphasis areas are particularly encouraged to apply but all areas of mechanical engineering will be considered. The senior appointment is intended for a scholar with a sustained record of research leadership who can help anchor major collaborative initiatives, mentor junior faculty and students, and lead center-scale or multi-investigator proposals for all areas of mechanical engineering; particular interest in autonomy, advanced materials, and manufacturing. The assistant professor appointment is intended for an emerging scholar with a compelling research vision and strong potential for externally funded scholarship for all areas of mechanical engineering; particular interest in energy materials, semiconductor materials, and advanced manufacturing. Requirements: Assistant Professor An earned doctorate in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related field by the anticipated start date. Evidence of research achievement and strong potential to develop an externally funded research program. Potential for excellence in undergraduate and graduate teaching and student mentoring. Commitment to interdisciplinary collaboration and service to the department, university, and profession. Associate or Full Professor An earned doctorate in Mechanical Engineering or a closely related field and qualifications appropriate for appointment at the rank of associate or full professor at UTEP. A sustained record of nationally or internationally recognized scholarship and externally funded research. Demonstrated excellence in teaching, graduate-student mentoring, and professional service. Evidence of research leadership, collaborative program development, and the ability to mentor faculty and students. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants should submit: (1) a cover letter describing their interest in the position and alignment with UTEP's mission; (2) a curriculum vitae; (3) a statement of research accomplishments and future plans; (4) a statement of teaching philosophy and interests; and (5) names and contact information for at least three professional references. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the positions are filled. Questions about these searches may be directed to James P. Carney, Ph.D., Search Committee Chair, at jpcarney@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205470": {
      "title": "Professor of Practice in Geotechnical Engineering",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Professor of Practice in Geotechnical Engineering The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Professor of Practice in Geotechnical Engineering Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Professor of Practice in Geotechnical Engineering Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Civil, Environmental, and Construction Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Oct 2, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 205470 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. Learn more about faculty life at UTEP and what it's like to live and work in El Paso: Life & Work in El Paso Job Description: The Department of Civil Engineering has 22 full-time faculty and 700 students enrolled in accredited BS in Civil Engineering, BS in Construction Engineering & Management, MS in Civil Engineering, MS in Construction Management, MS in Environmental Engineering, ME in Civil & Environmental Engineering, and PhD in Civil Engineering degree programs. The Department organizes and actively encourages its students to participate in its international programs, including the faculty-led study abroad programs to Brazil, Czech Republic, Mexico, Peru, as well as the Dual Master Degrees Program in Smart Cities with Czech Technical University. The civil engineering faculty members participate in research activities through the following centers affiliated with the Department: Center for Transportation Infrastructure Systems (CTIS); Center for Environmental Resource Management (CERM); NSF-Funded Engineering Research Center for Advancing Sustainability through Powered Infrastructure for Roadway Electrification (ASPIRE); USDOT-Funded Southern Plains Transportation Center (SPTC); USDOT-Funded Center for Efficient Mobility (CEM). The Department of Civil, Environmental and Construction Engineering (CECE) at the University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for a Professor of Practice in Geotechnical Engineering beginning January 2027. We seek an experienced geotechnical engineer with an M.S. or Ph.D. in civil engineering or a closely related field and at least 10 years of substantial professional experience. The successful candidate will connect professional geotechnical practice with graduate teaching and mentoring. Position Overview This position presents a unique opportunity to help shape the future of professional graduate education in geotechnical engineering. The successful candidate will play a leading role in the development and delivery of a practice-oriented online geotechnical engineering graduate program designed for working professionals. Responsibilities include developing and teaching in person and online graduate courses, integrating real-world engineering applications and case studies into the curriculum, mentoring students, and strengthening partnerships with industry and government agencies. The department seeks a candidate who can bridge industry practice and academic instruction while helping position CECE as a regional and national leader in professional education for geotechnical engineering. The candidate should be able to translate field investigation, analysis, design, and construction experience into clear, rigorous instruction. Program Vision and Strategic Importance Geotechnical engineering is fundamental to the planning, design, and construction of infrastructure systems, including shallow and deep foundations, retaining structures, embankments, slopes, ground improvement systems, tunnels, and transportation infrastructure. Demand for engineers with advanced geotechnical expertise especially in the area of reliability-based design, continues to grow as federal, state, and local investments in infrastructure expand. Many practicing engineers need graduate education compatible with full-time work. CECE aims to combine flexible course delivery with technically rigorous, practice-based instruction. CECE's fully online Master of Science in Construction Management program provides an existing model for graduate instruction designed for working professionals. Building on this model, CECE envisions an online master's degree in geotechnical engineering. The Professor of Practice will help develop the curriculum, teach courses, and support the program if approved. This initiative aligns with the College of Engineering's strategic priorities of: Expanding professional graduate education; Strengthening engagement with industry partners; Supporting regional and national workforce development needs; and Advancing excellence in infrastructure engineering education. Responsibilities The successful candidate will be expected to: Develop and teach graduate courses in geotechnical engineering in person and online, including courses designed for working professionals; Help develop and assess the proposed online graduate curriculum in geotechnical engineering; Use geotechnical design projects, field investigation data, case studies, and current engineering practice in instruction; Mentor graduate students, including practicing engineers pursuing advanced education; Build partnerships with geotechnical consulting firms, contractors, transportation agencies, and other infrastructure stakeholders; Support student recruitment, professional outreach, and workforce development initiatives; and Evaluate course outcomes and contribute to program growth and the department's educational mission. Requirements: Applicants must possess: An M.S. or Ph.D. in civil engineering or a closely related field with graduate coursework or professional specialization in geotechnical engineering; At least 10 years of substantial professional experience in geotechnical engineering, including responsibility for geotechnical investigation, analysis, design, or construction support; Evidence of effective teaching, technical training, or mentoring, or clear potential to teach at the graduate level; and Ability to explain technical material clearly and collaborate with students, faculty, and professional partners. Preference will be given to candidates with: Experience teaching at the university level or providing technical instruction to practicing engineers; Professional Engineer (PE) licensure, or eligibility and willingness to obtain licensure within three years of appointment; Experience leading geotechnical engineering work on transportation or other infrastructure projects; Experience in designing or teaching online courses; Established professional relationships with geotechnical consulting firms, public agencies, contractors, or infrastructure owners; and Experience developing graduate curricula or continuing education programs for practicing engineers. Additional Information: Appointment and Salary This is a 9-month, non-tenure-track Professor of Practice appointment with a competitive salary commensurate with candidate's qualifications and experience. In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Interested applicants should submit: Curriculum Vitae (CV); A statement describing teaching experience and approach to practice-oriented instruction; Names and contact information for at least three professional references. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=195857": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Prof. in Artificial Intelligence for Semiconductor & Robotic Engr. (2 Positions)",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Research Assistant Prof. in Artificial Intelligence for Semiconductor & Robotic Engr. (2 Positions) The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Research Assistant Prof. in Artificial Intelligence for Semiconductor & Robotic Engr. (2 Positions) Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Research Assistant Prof. in Artificial Intelligence for Semiconductor & Robotic Engr. (2 Positions) Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Industrial, Manufacturing, and Systems Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Dec 18, 2025 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 26-COEN-20 Position ID: 195857 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1 institution located at the U.S.-Mexico border, in the vibrant city of El Paso, Texas. UTEP is recognized nationally for its commitment to access, excellence, and impact, particularly as one of the largest Hispanic-serving universities in the United States. With an enrollment of nearly 25,000 students, 85% of whom are Hispanic and many of whom are first-generation college students, UTEP is dedicated to providing a transformative education. UTEP offers 171 bachelor's, master's, and doctoral degree programs, fostering innovation and intellectual growth across its nine colleges and schools. The university's unique location in the border region provides distinctive research and academic opportunities, with special strengths in areas such as bilingual education, Hispanic health disparities, environmental sustainability, and international trade. UTEP faculty members have earned more than $130 million in annual research expenditures, contributing to the university's growing reputation as a leader in public value research. El Paso's cultural diversity and geographical position make it an ideal location for collaboration and engagement in binational research and community partnerships. The city enjoys over 300 days of sunshine annually, a low cost of living, and offers a range of outdoor activities and cultural events, contributing to an outstanding quality of life for its residents. About the Industrial, Manufacturing, and Systems Engineering Department The Department of Industrial, Manufacturing, and Systems Engineering (IMSE) at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) https://www.utep.edu/engineering/imse/ is a dynamic department within the College of Engineering. The department offers one undergraduate degree: Bachelor of Science in Industrial and Systems Engineering(B.S.I.S.E), three master's programs: Master of  Science in Industrial Engineering (M.S.I.E), Master of  Science in Manufacturing Engineering (M.S.Mfg.E), and Master of  Science in Systems Engineering (M.S.S.E), and a graduate certificate in Systems Engineering (G.C.S.E). The Master of Science in Systems Engineering (M.S.S.E) is available in-person or fully online. The department has an enrollment of more than 160 undergraduate and over 120 graduate students. The IMSE faculty is actively involved in sponsored research, with annual expenditures exceeding $2.5 million. Research activities are conducted in multiple research laboratories at the Department and research centers in the College and the University. Job Description: The Department of Industrial, Manufacturing & Systems Engineering (IMSE) at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for two non-tenure-track Research Assistant Professors in AI for Semiconductor Manufacturing and/or AI Robotics. Focus areas include: yield/defect causal analysis and XAI; process-variation monitoring and root-cause analysis; intelligent scheduling/dispatching for WIP/throughput (multi-objective, real-time) and supply-chain optimization; PdM and RUL with health monitoring; digital twins/smart factories, cross-site transfer and federated/edge learning; uncertainty estimation and calibration (e.g., conformal prediction, risk-aware decisions); and robotics for fabs?AMR planning/tasking, robotic vision & multimodal perception, wafer/FOUP/tool handling, and RL/Sim2Real. The successful candidate will join a growing and dynamic department, contributing to research and service in alignment with UTEP's mission as an R1 institution and a Hispanic-serving university. Responsibilities: Research: Lead and collaborate on topics in yield and defect causal analysis, intelligent scheduling & multi-objective optimization (real-time dispatching under dynamic constraints), Virtual Metrology (VM), predictive maintenance (PdM) & remaining useful life (RUL), digital twins / smart manufacturing, and uncertainty calibration & trustworthy AI (e.g., conformal prediction). AI Robotics in Fabs: Apply perception, planning, and control to cleanroom logistics (AMR/AGV), vision-based inspection, equipment upkeep, and safe human-robot collaboration. Funding & Industry: Co-lead or lead proposals (NSF/DOE/DoD, state, industry); execute PoCs and tech transfer with foundries, equipment/materials/metrology vendors. Data & Platforms: Establish robust data governance and MLOps pipelines; develop reusable algorithms and prototype systems (including robotics perception/planning stacks). Mentoring: Supervise MS/PhD students; co-author high-impact publications. Service: Support program development, workshops/symposia, and departmental needs (including ABET support as appropriate). Requirements: A Ph.D. in Manufacturing Engineering or related fields, including Industrial, Mechanical, Aerospace, Electrical Engineering, and Computer Science. Commitment to undergraduate and graduate education. Demonstrated expertise in machine learning/deep learning and software development (Python; PyTorch/TensorFlow). Peer-reviewed publications and strong scientific writing skills; ability to draft competitive research proposals. Demonstrated potential for establishing an extramurally funded research program. Excellent communication skills. Preferred Qualifications (one or more): AI Robotics: ROS/ROS2, Python, perception (CV/multimodal), motion/task planning, control & safety, simulation (Gazebo/Isaac Sim), and edge deployment. Semiconductor manufacturing experience (front-/back-end, metrology/inspection, equipment maintenance, yield-improvement projects). Digital twin, IIoT, MLOps, real-time data streaming, edge computing. Causal/XAI, Bayesian methods, reinforcement learning, multi-objective optimization (mathematical programming, heuristics/meta-heuristics). Proven success as PI/co-PI or primary writer on large proposals (e.g., NSF/DOE/DoD/state/industry), and/or prior experience writing/executing Other Country NSTC/MOST grants or similar programs. Experience in course design, supervising students, and leading hands-on projects. Additional Information: Hiring decisions are based on budget approval. Salary will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants must submit the following: 1) a letter of interest describing their interest in the position and their commitment to the IMSE Department and UTEP mission; 2) a detailed curriculum vita; 3) name and complete contact information of at least three references; 4) research statement (up to 3 pages); 5) mentorship statement (up to 3 pages); 6) unofficial transcripts; and 7) copies of the candidate's two most representative publications. The mentoring statement should highlight their experiences and their vision for supporting student success and mentoring students. This statement should also suggest how they, as member of the UTEP IMSE faculty, will contribute to UTEP's mission of access to excellent higher education. UTEP hires and invests in faculty who value our culture of care and our students' success. Review of applications will start on January 5, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. For information/inquiries regarding the position, please email: btseng@utep.edu and clin@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=190578": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Professor in Microelectronics and Semiconductors (3 Positions)",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Research Assistant Professor in Microelectronics and Semiconductors (3 Positions) The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Research Assistant Professor in Microelectronics and Semiconductors (3 Positions) Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Research Assistant Professor in Microelectronics and Semiconductors (3 Positions) Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Electrical and Computer Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Jul 18, 2025 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 26-COEN-15 Position ID: 190578 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, opportunity, and community engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 25,000 students - 85% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 171 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two DOE national laboratories in the region and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its top designation as an R1 research university, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas and go on to earn a median salary of more than $100,000 10 years after graduation. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center and children's museum experiences. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. Texas has no income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is the fifth safest large city in the United States. In 2025, U.S. News and World Report named El Paso as the second best big city in the United States in which to live. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. ABOUT THE ECE DEPARTMENT The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at UTEP (http://ece.utep.edu) offers Bachelor of Science (B.S.) and Master of Science (M.S.) degrees in Electrical Engineering and in Computer Engineering, and a Ph.D. degree in Electrical and Computer Engineering. The department has an enrollment of over 700 undergraduate and graduate students. The ECE faculty is actively involved in sponsored research with annual expenditures of more than $4.9 million in fiscal year 2024. Research activities are carried out in multiple research laboratories at the Department and at research centers in the College of Engineering and the University. The ECE department has developed significant capabilities, which include faculty hires and development of laboratory and metrology infrastructure to support research, workforce development, and commercialization in microelectronics and semiconductors. The faculty in the area are currently conducting funded multidisciplinary semiconductor research in various areas of expertise. In one example, UTEP is the lead institution of a funded 5 year, Consortium for Electronics in Extreme Environments (E3C) comprised of three universities and three national labs with the mission to establish an enduring research capacity and a pipeline of highly skilled engineers ready to enter employment at national laboratories. In another example, UTEP is a partner in the $840M project funded by DARPA's Next Generation Microelectronics Manufacturing program led by the Texas Institute for Electronics to build a Microelectronics Manufacturing Center. Moreover, UTEP is a partner of the Scalable Asymmetric Lifecycle Enhancement (SCALE) network to train the workforce for microelectronics. Other significant external funding agencies include NNSA, DOE, NSA, Sandia National Labs, Intel, DARPA, and NSF. The ECE department manages a cleanroom facility designed for fabrication and characterization of semiconductors. The cleanroom is a 6,000 square-feet facility with 2,500 square-feet of class-100 and class-1000 space. Major fabrication capabilities include; nanoscale 3D printing, thin-film deposition, plasma etching, photolithography, oxidation, and diffusion. Major characterization capabilities include; scanning electron microscopy, optical digital microscopy, temperature-controlled Raman and photoluminescence, film thickness measurement via stylus profilometry and light interferometry, UV-vis-IR spectrophotometer, Hall-effect measurement, four-point probe for resistivity, deep-level transient spectroscopy, and a semiconductor parameter analyzer. Job Description: The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for three non-tenure-track Research Assistant Professor positions in the area of microelectronics and semiconductors. These positions are part of UTEP's strategic initiative to develop a world-class research cluster in this field, supported by the UT System Regents' Research Excellence Program. Research Assistant Professors will contribute to high-impact research and dissemination, help secure extramural research funding, support the management of research facilities, and engage in collaborative efforts across departments and institutions. This is a research-focused, non-tenure-track appointment. While formal teaching is not required, candidates will play a key role in mentoring and training students and research staff in a collaborative, interdisciplinary environment. Key Responsibilities Conduct and support cutting-edge research in microelectronics, semiconductors, or related areas. Develop proposals and help secure extramural funding to support sustainable research activity. Collaborate with faculty within UTEP's semiconductor cluster and with external partners (e.g., universities, national labs, and industry). Disseminate research findings through peer-reviewed publications, presentations, and reports. Contribute to the management and technical advancement of UTEP's semiconductor research and teaching facility, including: Stewardship of cleanroom and research infrastructure (e.g., fabrication and metrology equipment). Development of new technologies and process capabilities. Training of graduate and undergraduate students and visiting researchers. Oversight and continuous improvement of facility operations. Application materials should clearly demonstrate the candidate's ability to meet these expectations and contribute meaningfully to the growth of the semiconductor research cluster. Requirements: Ph.D. in electrical engineering, computer engineering, or a closely related field conducting research in microelectronics, semiconductors or related areas. Strong record of research productivity, as evidenced by peer-reviewed publications and contributions to funded projects. Demonstrated ability to contribute effectively to team-based research. Commitment to training and mentoring undergraduate and graduate students in a research setting. Excellent oral and written communication skills. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS At least 2 years of postdoctoral or equivalent experience in a research-intensive environment such as a university, national laboratory, or industry R&D. Hands-on experience with advanced semiconductor research infrastructure (e.g., cleanroom operations, lithography, deposition, etching, metrology). Demonstrated experience mentoring graduate and/or undergraduate students, or postdoctoral researchers. Experience in proposal writing. A track record of securing extramural funding. Additional Information: SALARY : Rank and salary are commensurate with qualifications and experience. Hiring decisions are based on budget approval. Funding will be provided for a three-year period. Appointments will be renewed annually depending on funding availability and performance. The successful applicant will be expected to secure external funding by year four of their appointment to support their research programs. In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, sexual orientation, or gender identity in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . Application Instructions: Applicants must submit the following: 1) a cover letter describing their interest in the position and how they meet position requirements; 2) a detailed curriculum vita; 3) research statement (up to 3 pages); 4) copies of the candidate's two most representative publications; and 4) name and complete contact information of at least three references. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applicants are encouraged to apply by September 1, 2025. For information/inquiries regarding the position, please email, ECE_Faculty_Search@utep.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=193873": {
      "title": "Research Assistant Professors in Advanced Manufacturing, AI, and Materials Design (3 Positions)",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Research Assistant Professors in Advanced Manufacturing, AI, and Materials Design (3 Positions) The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Research Assistant Professors in Advanced Manufacturing, AI, and Materials Design (3 Positions) Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Research Assistant Professors in Advanced Manufacturing, AI, and Materials Design (3 Positions) Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Aerospace Center Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Oct 15, 2025 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 26-COEN-16 Position ID: 193873 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: About The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, opportunity, and community-engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 25,000 students, 85% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 171 bachelor's, master's, and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Job Description: The Aerospace Center at UTEP invites applications for three Research Assistant Professor positions in Advanced Manufacturing, Artificial Intelligence, and Materials Design under the STAR (Space Technologies and AI-Driven Research) Frontier initiative. These positions are housed within the Aerospace Center. Successful candidates will advance and specialize in advanced manufacturing, contributing to the development of innovative fabrication techniques and connecting current projects across Centers and Departments to increase synergy and impact to the region and nation. The positions will concentrate on computational simulations and AI-driven tools, including materials discovery, design, property prediction, process optimization, and advanced simulations, to uncover novel materials tailored for space applications. Key Responsibilities Conduct research in materials, manufacturing, and AI integration. Develop and lead federal and industry proposals (NSF, DOE, DoD, NASA, and others). Publish impactful interdisciplinary research. Mentor graduate and undergraduate researchers. Collaborate with all departments and colleges as appropriate. Research Focus Areas AI/ML-Guided Materials Design and Discovery. Advanced Manufacturing and Process Optimization. Space and Harsh-Environment Materials Multifunctional and Bioinspired Systems. Requirements: Minimum: Ph.D. in Mechanical Engineering, Materials Science, Computer Science, or related field; expertise in additive manufacturing, materials design, or AI/ML; strong publication record. Additional Information: The initial salary is competitive, based on qualifications and experience, and comes with excellent fringe benefits. Hiring decisions are based on budget approval. In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Submit: (1) Cover letter (indicate relevant research area), (2) CV, (3) Research Statement (2-3 pages), (4) Contact info for three references. Applications reviewed immediately until filled. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=199382": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate Professor in AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (CS)",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Research Assistant/Associate Professor in AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (CS) The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Research Assistant/Associate Professor in AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (CS) Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Research Assistant/Associate Professor in AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (CS) Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Computer Science Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Apr 10, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: RREP-26-75 Position ID: 199382 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: ABOUT THE CS DEPARTMENT: UTEP's CS department is part of the College of Engineering, along with six other academic departments. The CS department is deeply committed to student professional development and advancement through faculty mentoring and research engagement at all levels. Leading companies and government agencies heavily recruit our graduates. The College of Engineering offers students interdisciplinary and industry training opportunities, study abroad programs, and other professional development opportunities. The college is consistently ranked among the top schools in the U.S. in awarding bachelor's degrees to Hispanics. The department offers B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. degrees in Computer Science, a new B.S. in Artificial Intelligence, and M.S. degrees in Software Engineering and Artificial Intelligence. The B.S. in CS offers concentrations in Software Engineering, Secure Cyber-Systems, and Data Analytics, with a Secure Cyber-Systems concentration also available at the graduate level. Through CS undergraduate and graduate programs, UTEP is designated as an NSA Center of Academic Excellence in Cyber Defense, Cyber Operations, and Cybersecurity Research. CS faculty lead impactful research in areas such as Artificial Intelligence, Cybersecurity, Software Engineering, Computer Systems, and Theoretical Foundations of CS. Our faculty are nationally recognized for their scholarship and national leadership in professional organizations, and the department consistently ranks among the highest at UTEP in research expenditures. With a vibrant academic community of 22 tenured and tenure-track faculty members and four teaching faculty, we foster innovation, collaboration, and student success. UTEP's recently established Applied AI Innovation Institute (AAII) offers a dynamic environment for collaborative, interdisciplinary AI research and deployment in complex, culturally rich settings, an asset for faculty seeking to make real-world impact. ABOUT UTEP AND EL PASO: The University of Texas at El Paso is a Carnegie R1 and Community Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. UTEP enrolls more than 25,000 students - about 85% of them Hispanic and more than 50% the first generation in their families to attend college. The University employs approximately 4,000 faculty, staff and students. It has one of the lowest out-of-pocket costs of any research university in the U.S., underscoring its commitment to offer an exceptional education at a great value. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, where three states across two countries converge along the Rio Grande, UTEP is a public research university that transforms lives. At UTEP, students have access to 171 bachelor's, master's, and doctoral degree programs in nine colleges and schools. UTEP advances discovery of public value and positively impacts the health, culture, education and economy of the community it serves. With more than $145 million in total annual research expenditures, UTEP ranks fifth in Texas for federal research expenditures at public universities and is one of the major economic engines in the region, adding more than $1.4 billion to the local economy annually. El Paso is a highly livable, bicultural community of more than 680,000 people that offers affordable homes and attractive neighborhoods. It has been repeatedly named among the safest large U.S. cities. El Paso experiences almost 300 days of sunshine annually, and residents enjoy outdoor activities year-round. El Paso's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with an award-winning dinner theater, music and dance productions, contemporary and student art galleries, and a natural history museum. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues. El Paso's professional sports teams include the El Paso Chihuahuas, a Triple-A Baseball team affiliated with the San Diego Padres, El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team in the USL Championship, and El Paso Rhinos, a professional ice-hockey team that plays in the North American Hockey League. Job Description: The Department of Computer Science (CS) of The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for non-tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Research Assistant/Associate Professor to begin immediately. This is a collaborative initiative between the Electrical and Computer Engineering and Computer Science Departments. A complementary faculty search is being conducted by the Electrical and Computer Engineering Department. The Departments are primarily interested in candidates who will contribute to the growth of research and academic programs in the emerging area of HW/SW design for AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (AIRCIS), including IoT devices, robotics, and drones. We are interested in candidates who can work on interdisciplinary teams, complement current expertise, and contribute to the Cyber Physical Systems and the Smart Systems interdisciplinary research focus areas at the UTEP College of Engineering through fundamental disciplinary and/or application-specific research. Priority will be given to the overall originality and promise of the candidate's work and potential to contribute to AIRCIS. Candidates will be expected to develop vigorous, externally funded research programs in their technical areas.  The application materials should clearly demonstrate the potential of the candidate to succeed in meeting these expectations. Two positions are available, one to be affiliated with the Department of Computer Science, and one to be affiliated with the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineers. Interested candidates should apply to each search individually to be considered for a position in that department. This posting is for a position in the Department of Computer Science. Applicants are encouraged to apply to the department that is a better fit for their expertise. Requirements: A Ph.D. in computer science, or a related field. Demonstrated scholarly achievements as evidenced by a track record of publications in areas relevant to AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (AIRCIS). Potential for establishing an extramurally funded research program. Excellent communication skills are required. A commensurate record of successful industrial/academic activities, including funded research, and scholarly publications, is required for applicants at the research associate professor level. Additional Information: Rank and salary are commensurate with qualifications and experience. Hiring decisions are based on budget approval. In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants must submit the following: 1) a letter of interest describing their interest in the position and their fit to the position requirements; 2) detailed curriculum vita; 3) name and complete contact information of at least three references; 4) research statement (up to 3 pages); 5) unofficial transcripts; and 6) copies of their two most representative publications. Please state in your cover letter which rank you wish to be considered for. The research statement should highlight the candidate's research accomplishments relevant to AIRCIS, as well as outline the research vision they plan to execute upon joining UTEP. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. For information/inquiries regarding the position, please email, cqlauter@utep.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=199381": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Associate Professor in AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (ECE)",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Research Assistant/Associate Professor in AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (ECE) The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Research Assistant/Associate Professor in AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (ECE) Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Research Assistant/Associate Professor in AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (ECE) Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Electrical and Computer Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Apr 10, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: RREP-26-74 Position ID: 199381 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: ABOUT THE ECE DEPARTMENT: The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering at UTEP ( http://ece.utep.edu ) offers two undergraduate and three graduate degrees: Bachelor of Science in Electrical Engineering (BSEE), Bachelor of Science in Computer Engineering (BSCpE), Master of Science in Electrical Engineering (MSEE.), Master of Science in a Computer Engineering (MSCpE), and a Ph.D. degree in Electrical and Computer Engineering. The department has an enrollment of over 800 students with 713 undergraduate and 92 graduate students (M.S. and Ph.D. students). The ECE faculty is actively involved in sponsored research with annual expenditures of more than $5.5 million. Research activities are carried out in multiple research laboratories at the Department and at research centers in the College and the University. Capabilities include an indoor motion capture arena with a Crazflie drone swarm, Quanser's robot studio with aerial and ground vehicles, and imaging systems, open for candidate engagement. ABOUT UTEP AND EL PASO: The University of Texas at El Paso is a Carnegie R1 and Community Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. UTEP enrolls more than 25,000 students - about 85% of them Hispanic and more than 50% the first generation in their families to attend college. The University employs approximately 4,000 faculty, staff and students. It has one of the lowest out-of-pocket costs of any research university in the U.S., underscoring its commitment to offer an exceptional education at a great value. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, where three states across two countries converge along the Rio Grande, UTEP is a public research university that transforms lives. At UTEP, students have access to 171 bachelor's, master's, and doctoral degree programs in nine colleges and schools. UTEP advances discovery of public value and positively impacts the health, culture, education and economy of the community it serves. With more than $145 million in total annual research expenditures, UTEP ranks fifth in Texas for federal research expenditures at public universities and is one of the major economic engines in the region, adding more than $1.4 billion to the local economy annually. El Paso is a highly livable, bicultural community of more than 680,000 people that offers affordable homes and attractive neighborhoods. It has been repeatedly named among the safest large U.S. cities. El Paso experiences almost 300 days of sunshine annually, and residents enjoy outdoor activities year-round. El Paso's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with an award-winning dinner theater, music and dance productions, contemporary and student art galleries, and a natural history museum. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues. El Paso's professional sports teams include the El Paso Chihuahuas, a Triple-A Baseball team affiliated with the San Diego Padres, El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team in the USL Championship, and El Paso Rhinos, a professional ice-hockey team that plays in the North American Hockey League. Job Description: The Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering (ECE) of The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for non-tenure-track faculty positions at the rank of Research Assistant/Associate Professor to begin immediately. This is a collaborative initiative between the Electrical and Computer Engineering and Computer Science Departments. A complementary faculty search is being conducted by the Computer Science Department. The Departments are primarily interested in candidates who will contribute to the growth of research and academic programs in the emerging area of HW/SW design for AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (AIRCIS), including IoT devices, robotics, and drones. We are interested in candidates who can work on interdisciplinary teams, complement current expertise, and contribute to the Cyber Physical Systems and the Smart Systems interdisciplinary research focus areas at the UTEP College of Engineering through fundamental disciplinary and/or application-specific research. Priority will be given to the overall originality and promise of the candidate's work and potential to contribute to AIRCIS. Candidates will be expected to develop vigorous, externally funded research programs in their technical areas.  The application materials should clearly demonstrate the potential of the candidate to succeed in meeting these expectations. Two positions are available, one to be affiliated with the Department of Computer Science, and one to be affiliated with the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineers. Interested candidates should apply to each search individually to be considered for a position in that department. This posting is for a position in the Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering. Applicants are encouraged to apply to the department that is a better fit for their expertise. Requirements: A Ph.D. in electrical/computer engineering, or a related field. Demonstrated scholarly achievements as evidenced by a track record of publications in areas relevant to AI-driven Resource Constrained Intelligent Systems (AIRCIS). Potential for establishing an extramurally funded research program. Excellent communication skills are required. A minimum of five years of relevant experience beyond degree completion or postdoctoral work and a commensurate record of successful industrial/academic activities, including funded research, and scholarly publications, is required for applicants at the research associate professor level. Additional Information: Rank and salary are commensurate with qualifications and experience. Hiring decisions are based on budget approval. In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants must submit the following: 1) a letter of interest describing their interest in the position and their fit to the position requirements; 2) detailed curriculum vita; 3) name and complete contact information of at least three references; 4) research statement (up to 3 pages); 5) unofficial transcripts; and 6) copies of their two most representative publications. Please state in your cover letter which rank you wish to be considered for. The research statement should highlight the candidate's research accomplishments relevant to AIRCIS, as well as outline the research vision they plan to execute upon joining UTEP. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. For information/inquiries regarding the position, please email, ECE_Faculty_Search@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
