    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=193879": {
      "title": "Research Associate Professor in Autonomous Systems for Space Technologies",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Research Associate Professor in Autonomous Systems for Space Technologies The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Research Associate Professor in Autonomous Systems for Space Technologies Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Research Associate Professor in Autonomous Systems for Space Technologies Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Aerospace Center Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Oct 15, 2025 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 26-COEN-17 Position ID: 193879 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, opportunity, and community engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 25,000 students, 85% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 171 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable, and Texas has no income tax. Job Description: UTEP invites applications for a Research Associate Professor (RAP) in Autonomous Systems for Space Technologies. This position is housed in the Aerospace Center and collaborates with all departments and colleges as appropriate. It is part of the STAR (Space Technologies and AI-Driven Research) Frontier initiative focusing on robotics, autonomous systems, enhancing the integration of AI and robotics into space technologies, AI for space exploration, unmanned aerial systems (UAS), and defense. Key Responsibilities Conduct research on autonomous systems integrating AI into aerospace platforms. Prototype autonomous systems for in-space manufacturing and testing. Develop AI algorithms for navigation, guidance, and control. Lead collaborative research proposals for NSF, DoD, DOE, NASA and with other academia, industry, and government. Mentor students and build interdisciplinary collaborations. Research Focus Areas Space Autonomy - Robotic systems for in-space manufacturing and exploration. Swarm & Cooperative Systems - Multi-agent coordination and distributed sensing. Aerospace Security - AI-integrated surveillance and autonomous detection. In-Situ Autonomy - Edge AI and adaptive control in GPS-denied environments. Autonomous Experimentation - Closed-loop AI-in-the-loop testing frameworks. Requirements: Minimum: Ph.D. in Aerospace, Mechanical, Electrical, or Computer Engineering, or related field; strong publication record; interdisciplinary experience. Additional Information: The initial salary is competitive, based on qualifications and experience, and comes with excellent fringe benefits. Hiring decisions are based on budget approval. In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Submit: (1) Cover letter, (2) CV, (3) Research Statement (2-3 pages), (4) Contact information for three references. Applications are reviewed immediately until filled. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=202760": {
      "title": "Research Professor",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Research Professor - Electrified Transportation Systems and Infrastructure The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Research Professor - Electrified Transportation Systems and Infrastructure Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Research Professor - Electrified Transportation Systems and Infrastructure Category: Engineering Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Civil Engineering Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Jul 17, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: RREP-26-77 Position ID: 202760 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1 and community-engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance the discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls 26,000 students - 85% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's, and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's border location makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, such as bilingual education/language acquisition, Hispanic health disparities, border environment and immigration, environmental sustainability and infrastructure, and international trade and commerce. El Paso's cultural diversity and geographical position make it an ideal location for collaboration and engagement in binational research and community partnerships. The city enjoys over 300 days of sunshine annually, a low cost of living, and offers a range of outdoor activities and cultural events, contributing to an outstanding quality of life for its residents. The College of Engineering is among the nation's leading producers of Hispanic engineers and offers exceptional opportunities for interdisciplinary research and innovation. About ASPIRE ASPIRE is a 4th-generation National Science Foundation Engineering Research Center (NSF-ERC) advancing infrastructure and systems solutions to electrify the transportation sector. With headquarters at Utah State University (USU), ASPIRE brings together UTEP and nine other academic institutions, including an international collaborator. ASPIRE is organized around four major thrust areas: Data (data analysis and fusion, AI and optimization, cybersecurity, IoT, networks), Adoption (user acceptance, public policy, economic impact), Power (power systems, grid-integrated charging systems, battery systems), and Transportation (systems and infrastructure). These areas are organized into four integrated projects with tasks led by interdisciplinary, multi-institutional teams: Charging Stations of the Future, Electrified Roadways, Integrated Systems, and Pathways. ASPIRE pioneers the integration of the transportation and electric utility infrastructure into an integrated system. Since its launch in 2020, ASPIRE has secured $192M in funding including $25.3M in Year 5 alone and expanded its Electric Vehicle Roadway (EVR) facility at USU with an infusion of $15M from the state of Utah. This facility enables full systems-level research, demonstration, and validation, already leveraged by the Society of Automotive Engineers (SAE) to evaluate commercial and pre-commercial technologies from industry partners. ASPIRE has an extensive innovation ecosystem that includes over 70 industry members from varied sectors including local government, industry, and international academic institutions. Examples of ASPIRE breakthroughs in partnerships with our industry partners include: The world's first 1 megawatt wireless charger for heavy-duty trucks, piloted at the Utah Inland Port and ASPIRE EVR testbed with USU, Kenworth, PacifiCorp and UPS. The world's first high-power, three-phase Dynamic Wireless Power Transfer (DWPT) system for Class 8 trucks demonstrated at highway speeds on US 52/231 from Purdue University with AECOM, P.C. Krause and Associates, and Cummins Inc. and Indiana Department of Transportation (INDoT). A growing IP portfolio with 39 patents and 17 more in the pipeline. The ASPIRE program has recently become the ASPIRE Foundation, a wholly owned nonprofit of USU. The Foundation serves as the link between university partner innovation and societal impact. ASPIRE plans to champion national initiatives in technology and workforce development and to support large-scale technology deployment across the country. Job Description: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) seeks an outstanding scholar and leader to advance high-impact research within the NSF ASPIRE Engineering Research Center (ERC), a fourth-generation NSF Engineering Research Center focused on intelligent infrastructure and systems at the transportation-grid edge. ASPIRE advances wireless charging technologies, intelligent transportation systems, cybersecurity, workforce development, and stakeholder engagement to accelerate the transition to a more sustainable transportation future. As a core ASPIRE institution and with support from the University of Texas Board of Regents, UTEP seeks to recruit an outstanding research faculty member to expand interdisciplinary research, foster strategic partnerships, and advance the deployment of transportation electrification and infrastructure technologies across Texas, the Southwest, and beyond. The University of Texas at El Paso seeks a highly qualified research professor to join the College of Engineering. With support from the University of Texas System Board of Regents, UTEP seeks to recruit an outstanding research leader to advance research in transportation electrification, electric vehicle infrastructure, and intelligent transportation systems. The successful candidate will help expand UTEP's leadership within the NSF ASPIRE Engineering Research Center by fostering interdisciplinary collaboration, strengthening strategic partnerships, and accelerating the deployment and adoption of electrified transportation technologies across Texas and the Southwest region. The successful candidate will develop and lead a nationally recognized research program focused on high-technology readiness level (TRL) research, demonstration, validation, and deployment activities that advance ASPIRE's mission. The faculty member will collaborate with researchers across ASPIRE partner institutions, industry members, utilities, transportation agencies, and community stakeholders to accelerate the transition of transportation electrification technologies from research to real-world implementation. This position offers a unique opportunity to advance high-TRL transportation electrification research within one of the nation's premier engineering research centers while leveraging a broad network of universities, industry, and government partners. Responsibilities Develop and sustain an internationally recognized research program in transportation electrification and related fields. Lead high-TRL research, demonstration, validation, and deployment activities aligned with the ASPIRE mission. Pursue and secure extramural funding from federal, state, and private-sector sources. Build partnerships with industry, utilities, transportation agencies, national laboratories, and community stakeholders in partnership with ASPIRE. Collaborate with ASPIRE researchers across participating institutions. Mentor undergraduate students, graduate students, postdoctoral researchers, and research staff. Support workforce development and community engagement initiatives. Requirements: Minimum Requirements An earned doctorate in Civil Engineering, Electrical Engineering, Industrial Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, Transportation Engineering, Materials Engineering, Energy Systems Engineering, or a closely related field. Demonstrated record of scholarly achievement, external funding success, ecosystem stakeholder engagement, and strong leadership and communication skills. Experience in validation, prototyping, demonstration, integration, and transition to operational use of technology. Preferred Qualifications National or international recognition in a relevant field; experience leading multidisciplinary research programs; experience with federally funded research centers or public-private partnerships; commitment to mentoring; and a strong network of research, industry, and government collaborators. Additional Information: Salary and benefits are highly competitive and commensurate with qualifications, experience, and rank. UTEP offers an attractive compensation package and significant opportunities for research growth and leadership. In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants must submit: (1) cover letter; (2) curriculum vitae; (3) research and strategic vision statement; (4) statement describing stakeholder engagement, interdisciplinary collaboration, and partnership-building experience; (5) mentoring statement; and (6) contact information for at least three professional references. Applications should be submitted through www.utep.edu/employment. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. For information/inquiries regarding the position, please email Dr. Ivonne Santiago at isantiago@utep.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205974": {
      "title": "Associate Professor or Professor and Chair of the Department of Social Work",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Associate Professor or Professor and Chair of the Department of Social Work The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Associate Professor or Professor and Chair of the Department of Social Work Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Associate Professor or Professor and Chair of the Department of Social Work Category: Health Sciences Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Social Work Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Oct 20, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-CoHS-04 Position ID: 205974 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. Learn more about faculty life at UTEP and what it's like to live and work in El Paso: Life & Work in El Paso Job Description: The Department: The Department of Social Work has educated social workers for more than four decades. The BSW and MSW programs are accredited by the Council on Social Work Education. The MSW concentration in Social Work in the Border Region grounds advanced practice in the social, cultural, and structural conditions of border communities, while the online MSW extends access to graduate education. The student body includes a Hispanic, bilingual majority and many first-generation and international students. The department offers its next Chair an opportunity to strengthen program quality, support student achievement, and guide growth through a collaborative vision. Departmental faculty conduct research in behavioral health, child welfare, migration and refugee experiences, intimate partner violence, aging, and health disparities. Community-engaged teaching, research, and service connect social work education with regional needs and support partnerships across the El Paso and Ciudad Juárez border. Faculty also contribute to the College's PhD in Interdisciplinary Health Sciences through doctoral mentoring, dissertation committee service, and dissertation direction. The next Chair will build on these strengths, support faculty development and research collaboration, and expand the department's visibility and impact. The department's website can be found at: https://www.utep.edu/chs/sw/ The College: The College of Health Sciences prepares the next generation of health and human service professionals to improve quality of life and address health disparities in their communities. The College offers five undergraduate degrees in Clinical Laboratory Science, Health Promotion, Rehabilitation Sciences, Social Work, and Kinesiology.  There are four master's degrees in the college: Speech Language and Hearing Sciences, Public Health, Social Work, and Kinesiology. There are currently three doctoral programs in the College: Doctor of Physical Therapy, Occupational Therapy Doctorate, and a PhD in Interdisciplinary Health Sciences. High impact educational experiences enable students to develop leadership, problem-solving, and critical thinking skills, as well as a strong sense of social responsibility through community and global engagement opportunities. Faculty and students engage in teaching, research, and service incorporating Boyer's Model of the scholarship of discovery, integration, application/engagement, and teaching and learning, all related to the applied health sciences ( https://www.utep.edu/chs/ ). The College's facilities and programs are designed to promote interprofessional teaching, learning, research, and community & global engagement initiatives. The College of Health Sciences enjoys a strong and collaborative relationship with a range of local, national, and global community partners.  Graduate and undergraduate students participate actively with clinical and research faculty mentors in conducting a variety of projects relevant to addressing health inequities and improving quality of life in the Paso del Norte region and beyond. The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) Department of Social Work in the College of Health Sciences invites applications for Associate Professor/Professor and Chair of the Department of Social Work, with an anticipated start date in mid-August 2027. We seek an accomplished social work scholar and experienced academic leader who will provide collaborative leadership, advance the department's teaching and research mission, and strengthen its community and binational partnerships. Candidates must present a record of scholarship, teaching, and service commensurate with appointment at the rank of Associate Professor/Professor and eligibility for tenure at UTEP. Located in the El Paso-Ciudad Juárez binational region, the Department of Social Work prepares practitioners and scholars to respond to the strengths and needs of border communities and beyond. The next Chair will build on this regional identity while advancing social work education, student success, research, and community engagement. The department offers a Bachelor of Social Work (BSW) preparing students for generalist practice and a Master of Social Work (MSW) preparing students for advanced practice with concentration in Social Work in the Border Region. The online MSW program expands access to graduate social work education. The Chair will guide the continued development of these programs in collaboration with program directors, faculty, staff, students, and community partners. Reporting to the Dean of the College of Health Sciences, the Chair will provide strategic direction and oversee departmental academic and administrative operations. Responsibilities include collaborative strategic planning; budget and resource management; faculty and staff recruitment, supervision, and evaluation; faculty mentoring and support for promotion and tenure; curriculum and program development; Council on Social Work Education (CSWE) accreditation and outcomes assessment; and coordination of teaching, advising, and field education with program leaders. The Chair will promote student recruitment, retention, and degree completion; strengthen externally funded research and interdisciplinary collaboration; cultivate community, agency, alumni, and binational partnerships; and represent the department within and beyond UTEP. The successful candidate will support shared governance, communicate transparently, manage conflict constructively, and sustain a respectful and productive workplace while maintaining an active scholarly agenda and contributing to teaching and mentoring. Requirements: PhD in Social Work, DSW, or earned doctorate in a closely related field. An MSW from a CSWE-accredited program. A minimum of two years of post-MSW experience as a practicing social worker. A sustained record of scholarly publications, teaching effectiveness, and professional and institutional service commensurate with appointment at the rank of Associate Professor/Professor and eligibility for tenure at UTEP. Demonstrated research productivity and experience securing or leading externally funded research, with expertise relevant to mental/behavioral health, health disparities, policy practice, and/or community-based research. Demonstrated successful academic leadership and administrative experience as a department chair, program director, associate chair, or in a comparable role, including responsibility for personnel, budgets or resource allocation, and academic planning. Demonstrated ability to lead collaboratively through shared governance, mentor faculty and staff, support student success, and work effectively with communities whose backgrounds and experiences reflect the border region. Licensure or eligibility for licensure as an LMSW or LCSW. Strong communication, organizational, and interpersonal skills; demonstrated sound judgment, constructive conflict management, and a commitment to social work ethics, social justice, and a respectful learning and working environment. Preferred Qualifications Experience as a department chair or in a comparable academic administrative role with responsibility for strategic planning, budget management, personnel evaluation, and faculty development. Experience leading CSWE accreditation, reaffirmation, curriculum development, and assessment of student learning and program outcomes. Bilingual proficiency in English and Spanish and experience building binational or international academic and community partnerships. Experience leading BSW and MSW programs, including online graduate education, field education partnerships, and initiatives supporting recruitment, retention, and degree completion. Experience in a Hispanic-Serving Institution and a research-intensive university; experience mentoring graduate and doctoral students and supporting faculty advancement and interdisciplinary research. A record of developing community-engaged scholarship, securing external resources, and cultivating partnerships with agencies, alumni, donors, and other stakeholders to advance departmental priorities. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants must submit the following information: Letter of application describing interest in serving as Associate Professor/Professor and Department Chair and explaining how the applicant meets the required and preferred qualifications. The letter should present a leadership philosophy and vision for the department, with specific examples of administrative accomplishments in strategic planning, personnel and budget management, faculty development, accreditation or program assessment, student success, and community partnerships. Curriculum vitae. Abbreviated research philosophy and plan (2 pages or less). Brief teaching philosophy (2 pages or less). A sample of research publications or scholarship productivity; and Names and contact information of three references (references will not be contacted until an applicant has been selected for an on-campus visit). Review of applications will begin immediately. Preliminary interviews will be conducted by video conference call Fall semester 2026 with on-campus finalist visits to follow. The position will remain open until filled. The successful applicant will commence duties mid-August 2027. Contact Information: Dr. Hyejin Jung Associate Professor Search Committee Chair UTEP Department of Social Work. hjung2@utep.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
