    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205301": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Multimedia Journalism",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Multimedia Journalism The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Multimedia Journalism Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor of Multimedia Journalism Category: Liberal Arts Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Communication Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 29, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-CLA-04 Position ID: 205301 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. Job Description: The Department of Communication, along with the Sam Donaldson Center for Communication, aims to be a site of creation and innovation through instruction, practice, and research. It serves as the nexus among communication scholars and practitioners from the United States, Mexico, and border communities. It is also a place of continuing education for Latina/o journalists, film makers, advertisers, and other communication practitioners, as well as the source of educational opportunity for students interested in attending a university in a bilingual, bicultural setting. The department seeks to enhance the educational aspirations of all students interested in bicultural/intercultural communication in a variety of contexts. The mission of the UTEP Department of Communication is to educate future leaders of communication organizations and industries and outstanding teachers in the field of communication as well as communication practitioners in multimedia journalism, video production, advertising, and organizational/corporate communication.  Research faculty also strive to produce internationally and nationally recognized research in multicultural contexts. With nearly 7,000 students and more than 20 departments and programs, the College of Liberal Arts is the largest college at UTEP. The college offers 36 bachelor's, 24 master's, and four Ph.D. degrees in the fine and performing arts, the humanities, and the social and behavioral sciences. The college also provides most of the UTEP core curriculum to students in all colleges. The college's undergraduate and graduate programs house many excellent faculty who have been recognized as national and international leaders in their fields. Faculty conduct interdisciplinary research in Latinx culture, health disparities, social justice, border studies, migration, environmental communication, and numerous other fields that enrich our bicultural and binational region. Under the UTEP EDGE, faculty and students across the college engage in a variety of high-impact practices including community engagement and research on both sides of the border, study abroad/away, internships, and capstone experiences that help prepare students for lifelong success. The college includes a bilingual MFA in Creative Writing, the first of its kind in the country; doctoral degrees in History, Rhetoric and Composition, Sociology, and Psychology; and the College of Liberal Arts Honors Program. UTEP is also home to the Patti and Paul Yetter Law School Preparation Institute, a university-wide program that serves students from all academic disciplines. The Department of Communication at The University of Texas at El Paso is seeking to hire a tenure-track Assistant Professor with expertise in multimedia journalism, broadcast and media production, effective Fall 2027. The successful candidate will contribute to the department's Multimedia Journalism and Digital Media Production programs and help advance innovative, professionally grounded approaches to journalism and media education. Applicants should be able to teach undergraduate and graduate courses in one or more of the following areas: broadcast and video journalism, multimedia writing, live and studio production, documentary and nonfiction storytelling, podcast production, digital audio and video production, investigative reporting and data journalism, visual storytelling, social media production, and emerging storytelling platforms and technologies. The successful candidate will be expected to contribute to the continued development of the department's applied journalism and digital media production curriculum and experiential learning opportunities. Of particular interest are candidates who can connect professional practice, teaching, and research and/or creative scholarship and who can help students produce high-quality journalism and media for public audiences across multiple platforms. The position also offers opportunities to develop Borderzine, the multimedia journalism program's bilingual, student-powered community news publication and its multimedia and broadcast news production, as well as to develop documentary, audio, digital and other public-facing media projects. The department welcomes candidates interested in building collaborations across communication programs and with other disciplines throughout the university. Requirements: Applicants must have a Ph.D. in journalism, communication or a related field, and a record of exceptional professional accomplishment in journalism and media production appropriate to a tenure-track appointment. Candidates must demonstrate significant professional expertise in broadcast, multimedia and digital storytelling and the potential to establish and sustain a program of research and/or creative scholarship appropriate for tenure and promotion. Candidates should demonstrate the ability to teach and mentor students in applied journalism and media production and to connect contemporary professional practices with the classroom. Preference will be given to candidates with a distinguished record of professional journalism and media production and recognized expertise in one or more areas such as broadcast and video journalism, live or studio production, documentary or nonfiction film, audio and podcasting, multimedia storytelling, and digital and emerging storytelling platforms. Bilingual English-Spanish proficiency is preferred and experience producing journalism or media for bilingual, Spanish-speaking or border communities. Additional preferred qualifications include evidence of industry leadership or innovation; professional recognition; experience supervising or mentoring student journalists and media producers; and demonstrated or potential contributions to research and/or creative scholarship, including peer-reviewed research, documentary or multimedia projects, investigative or public-interest journalism, innovative digital storytelling, externally funded projects, or other professionally recognized creative work. Experience developing industry and community partnerships, securing external funding, working with public-facing or community journalism initiatives, and experimenting with new approaches to audience engagement and media sustainability is also valued. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants must electronically submit: (1) a letter of interest describing their qualifications for the position, areas of teaching expertise, professional experience, and research and/or creative scholarship agenda; (2) curriculum vitae or professional résumé/CV; (3) examples of research, professional journalism, media production, or creative work appropriate to the candidate's background; (4) evidence of teaching effectiveness, if available; (5) graduate transcript (unofficial is acceptable for application; official documentation will be required as appropriate at hiring); and (6) names and full contact information for three references whom the search committee may contact. Candidates invited for campus visits may be asked to provide additional examples of scholarly, professional, or creative work and letters of recommendation. If you have questions about the position, please contact the Job Search Committee Chair, Sabiha Khan at skhan2@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205416": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Piano Pedagogy and Group Piano",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Piano Pedagogy and Group Piano The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Piano Pedagogy and Group Piano Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor of Piano Pedagogy and Group Piano Category: Liberal Arts Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Music Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Oct 1, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-CLA-06 Position ID: 205416 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. Learn more about faculty life at UTEP and what it's like to live and work in El Paso: Life & Work in El Paso Job Description: An accredited member of the National Association of Schools of Music, the UTEP Department of Music enrolls more than 300 music majors in undergraduate and graduate programs in performance, music education, conducting, theory and composition, commercial music, and music technology. Facilities include a 450-seat performance hall, the Digital Keyboard Laboratory, recording studios, computer and practice labs, and teaching spaces. The Department maintains professional collaborations with the El Paso Symphony Orchestra, El Paso Winds, El Paso Opera, and El Paso Pro-Musica, while the broader UTEP campus supports faculty research, creative activity, and professional development. The UTEP Piano Area supports undergraduate and graduate study in piano performance, piano pedagogy, music education, and related fields. As a Hispanic-Serving Institution located in the El Paso-Juárez U.S.-Mexico border region, UTEP serves students from a wide range of educational, cultural, and musical backgrounds. This distinctive setting offers meaningful opportunities for piano pedagogy, teacher preparation, community engagement, and professional partnerships within the region and beyond. The successful candidate will have the opportunity to help shape a contemporary piano pedagogy program connecting teacher preparation, group piano, keyboard technology, scholarship, and community engagement. The Department of Music at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Piano Pedagogy and Group Piano, beginning Fall 2027. We seek an imaginative, collaborative, and forward-thinking piano pedagogue whose primary professional identity is rooted in piano teaching, pedagogy scholarship, group-piano instruction, and the preparation of future piano teachers. This position offers an opportunity to lead and develop UTEP's Piano Pedagogy and Group Piano programs and to advance innovative teaching, scholarship, teacher preparation, and professional development through UTEP's Digital Keyboard Laboratory. The successful candidate's primary teaching and scholarly focus will be piano pedagogy; performance activity is welcomed as a complementary dimension of the candidate's professional profile. UTEP's Digital Keyboard Laboratory is a distinctive, state-of-the-art facility with 20 student keyboard stations, a teacher console, an acoustic piano, interactive teaching tools, observation capabilities, and specialized pedagogy resources. The laboratory provides a platform for technology-enhanced group piano, supervised teacher preparation, pedagogical research, workshops, professional development, and innovative instructional practices. The successful candidate will have significant freedom to help shape the laboratory's future use and visibility within the profession. Primary Responsibilities Teach and develop UTEP's undergraduate Piano Pedagogy sequence and teach graduate-level Piano Pedagogy as appropriate.  Teaching Assistants may also be included in this group. Coordinate and contribute to UTEP's four-semester Group/Class Piano sequence for music majors, including curriculum, proficiency expectations, assessment, and instructional continuity. Mentor students in individual and group-piano teaching through observation, supervised teaching, practica, and other experiential learning; mentor graduate research and teaching as appropriate. Provide pedagogical leadership for the Digital Keyboard Laboratory and advance its use for teaching, teacher preparation, research, workshops, and professional development. Establish and sustain a scholarly and creative agenda centered in piano pedagogy and consistent with UTEP expectations for tenure. Scholarly activity may include peer-reviewed publications, conference presentations, books or book chapters, pedagogical research, curricular or technology-based scholarship, professional resources, community-engaged scholarship, creative activity, or other nationally or internationally recognized contributions. Establish and advise a UTEP MTNA Collegiate Chapter and promote meaningful student participation in MTNA, TMTA, and EPMTA. Build productive relationships with independent and collegiate piano teachers and professional organizations through outreach, workshops, professional development, and other collaborative activities. Participate in recruitment, auditions, and mentoring of piano students in Pedagogy, Performance, Music Education, and related programs, and contribute to departmental and university service. Requirements: Earned doctorate in Piano Pedagogy, Piano Performance, Music Education, or a closely related field by the time of appointment. Demonstrated effectiveness in piano teaching and preparation for collegiate instruction in Piano Pedagogy, Group/Class Piano, or both. Demonstrated substantial preparation and engagement in piano pedagogy, evidenced through graduate study, collegiate teaching, teacher preparation, pedagogical research or scholarship, professional presentations, curriculum development, or related activity, with strong potential for sustained scholarly activity in the field. Knowledge of contemporary approaches to individual and group-piano teaching, teacher preparation, and technology-enhanced keyboard instruction. Keyboard proficiency appropriate to collegiate piano pedagogy and group-piano instruction, together with strong communication, organizational, and collaborative skills. Preferred Qualifications Graduate specialization or significant advanced study in Piano Pedagogy. Experience teaching or coordinating collegiate Group/Class Piano, Piano Pedagogy, or a related keyboard curriculum. Experience with digital keyboard laboratories, group-piano technology, or other technology-enhanced teaching environments. Experience mentoring developing piano teachers, graduate teaching assistants, or student research. Experience with professional music-teaching organizations, collegiate student chapters, teacher networks, or professional-development activities for independent and collegiate piano teachers. Evidence of initiative and creativity in building programs, partnerships, professional-development activities, or community-engaged projects. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants should submit the following through the UTEP employment system: Cover letter addressing interest in the position and relevant teaching, scholarly, and professional experience. Curriculum vitae. Names and contact information for three professional references. A one- to two-page statement describing the candidate's teaching philosophy and vision for Piano Pedagogy, Group Piano, and the pedagogical use of a Digital Keyboard Laboratory. Evidence of teaching effectiveness or teaching potential, such as sample syllabi, course materials, teaching evaluations, student-learning materials, or video examples. A sample or portfolio of current or emerging scholarly activity in piano pedagogy, such as a publication, conference proposal or presentation, dissertation or thesis excerpt, curriculum or research project, technology-based project, pedagogical resource, or comparable work. Optional links to representative performance materials may be included. Review of applications will begin November 2, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. For questions about the position, contact Dr. Dena Kay Jones, UTEP Piano Area Coordinator, dkjones@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204111": {
      "title": "Associate or Full Professor of Security Studies",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Associate or Full Professor of Security Studies The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Associate or Full Professor of Security Studies Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Associate or Full Professor of Security Studies Category: Liberal Arts Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Criminal Justice and Security Studies Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Aug 25, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-CLA-01 Position ID: 204111 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. The Department offers an online B.A. in Security Studies, a Master of Science in Intelligence and National Security, and a Master of Defense and Strategic Studies. The department also offers a B.A. in Criminal Justice, an equivalent 100 percent online B.A., and a Master of Science in Criminology and Criminal Justice. The department offers certificates in Intelligence and National Security Studies and Open-Source Intelligence. Job Description: The Department of Criminal Justice & Security Studies (CJ&SS) at the University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is seeking to fill one full-time position in Intelligence and National Security Studies beginning in Fall 2027 at the Associate or Full Professor level. Responsibilities include conducting and publishing scholarly research, seeking extramural funding, teaching face-to-face and online courses, and service to the department, university, and profession. Requirements: Applicants must have a Ph.D. from an accredited university in security studies, political science, public policy, history, psychology, criminal justice, or a closely related discipline. ABD applicants will be considered if a doctorate degree will be in hand by Fall 2027. An active research agenda that includes scholarly publications is required to apply. Applicants possessing only a J.D., Psy.D., or Ed.D. will not be considered. Preferred Qualifications: Areas of expertise are open, but preference will be given to candidates with expertise and a research agenda in advanced technologies in intelligence and national security, broadly conceived. This primarily includes influence operations and disinformation; cyber warfare, cyber operations, cyber security, or cybercrime; Artificial Intelligence (AI) policy; and possibly others. It also includes the technical aspects of collection and analysis, especially SIGINT, GEOINT, MASINT, or technical collection in contested environments. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants must electronically submit: (1) a letter of interest that includes teaching areas and research expertise, (2) curriculum vita (please use APA format when listing publications), (3) one scholarly publication, (4) a one-page research plan for the next 3 years; and (5) the names and full contact information of three references that we may contact. If you have questions about the position, please contact the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Misty Duke at mcduke@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205007": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor of Border Studies",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Associate/Full Professor of Border Studies The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Associate/Full Professor of Border Studies Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Associate/Full Professor of Border Studies Category: Liberal Arts Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Sociology and Anthropology Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 18, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-CLA-10 Position ID: 205007 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. Job Description: The Department of Sociology and Anthropology, which houses the Center for Inter-American and Border Studies, is one of the foundational departments in the field of border studies. We offer a BA in Anthropology and in Sociology, an MA and PhD in Sociology (in which all disciplines participate), and undergraduate courses in Geography. Our PhD program has a focus on Culture and Health and Borders and Mobility. The department boasts a vibrant intellectual and collegial atmosphere with ample opportunities for collaboration and growth. The Center for Inter-American and Border Studies (CIBS) is a hub for intellectual, scholarly and community-based research on the border region and Latin America as a whole. Given the department's proximity to Mexico, history of collaboration across the border and UTEP's unique student body, CIBS is a key unit on campus. We are looking for a leader in border studies research connected to the U.S. - Mexico border--someone who can collaborate across a broad range of projects and constituencies and is comfortable with interdisciplinary collaboration. The ideal candidate will be an accomplished border scholar with a strong record of publication and external funding to join the faculty of the Sociology and Anthropology department. The candidate will teach and mentor students in our doctoral program. A strong preference will be given to scholars with expertise and focus on the U.S. - Mexico border, but we are open to other regions. Other expertise could include border communities, labor, environment, and migration among others. In addition to their role as a tenured member of the Sociology and Anthropology Department, the faculty member will serve as the director of the state-funded Center for Inter-American and Border Studies. We seek someone with innovative ideas to drive border studies forward and grow CIBS. CIBS provides stimulation and collaboration in border studies across the university, binational community, Texas and Chihuahua, and globally. Requirements: The candidate will possess a PhD in Sociology, Anthropology, Geography or a related discipline. Candidates will have an extensive demonstrated record of extramural funding, publications and collaborations. Administrative experience is desired but is not required. We expect successful candidates to have an outstanding record commensurate with appointment at the Full or Associate Professor rank. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Cover Letter; Please indicate which rank you want to be considered Curriculum vitae Contact information for at least three professional references Application review will start Oct. 31, 2026 and continue until the position is filled. Please contact search committee chair Dr. Josiah Heyman ( jmheyman@utep.edu ) if you have any questions. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205269": {
      "title": "Associate/Full Professor of Rhetorical Theory",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Associate/Full Professor of Rhetorical Theory The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Associate/Full Professor of Rhetorical Theory Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Associate/Full Professor of Rhetorical Theory Category: Liberal Arts Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Communication Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 28, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-CLA-03 Position ID: 205269 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. Job Description: The UTEP program in Communication is a contemporary blend of the humanistic and applied directions of the field. The Department offers five areas of concentration: Communication Studies, Digital Media Production, Organizational and Corporate Communication, Multimedia Journalism, and Media Advertising. We also offer minors in Communication Studies and Film Studies.  Our faculty includes a number of highly recognized experts in the various Communication subfields. The Department of Communication is housed in the College of Liberal Arts. With nearly 7,000 students and more than 20 departments and programs, the College of Liberal Arts is the largest college at UTEP. The college offers 36 bachelor's, 24 master's, and four Ph.D. degrees in the fine and performing arts, the humanities, and the social and behavioral sciences. The college also provides most of the UTEP core curriculum to students in all colleges. The college's undergraduate and graduate programs house many excellent faculty who have been recognized as national and international leaders in their fields. Faculty conduct interdisciplinary research in Latinx culture, health disparities, social justice, border studies, migration, environmental communication, and numerous other fields that enrich our bicultural and binational region. Under the UTEP EDGE, faculty and students across the college engage in a variety of high-impact practices including community engagement and research on both sides of the border, study abroad/away, internships, and capstone experiences that help prepare students for lifelong success. The college includes a bilingual MFA in Creative Writing, the first of its kind in the country; doctoral degrees in History, Rhetoric and Composition, Sociology, and Psychology; and the College of Liberal Arts Honors Program. UTEP is also home to the Patti and Paul Yetter Law School Preparation Institute, a university-wide program that serves students from across all academic disciplines. The Department of Communication is seeking to hire a tenured Associate Professor or Professor with a research and teaching emphasis in Rhetorical Theory and Communication, effective Fall 2027. Applicants should be able to teach undergraduate and graduate courses in one or more of the following areas: rhetoric and/or rhetorical/communication theory, international/intercultural communication, Latina/o/x communication studies, film studies, cultural studies, or other areas in the candidate's area of expertise. Requirements: Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Communication, Rhetoric, or a closely related field (with a research concentration in Rhetoric) from an accredited university. An active research agenda and published scholarly journal articles and/or books are required. The potential to secure external funding is highly valued. For appointment at the rank of Associate Professor, applicants must demonstrate: A sustained record of significant scholarly achievement and peer-reviewed publication. A record of effective undergraduate or graduate teaching and student mentorship. A record of obtaining external research support. Accomplishments sufficient to meet UTEP's requirements for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor with tenure. For appointment at the rank of Professor, applicants must also demonstrate: A sustained and distinguished record of scholarly achievement and national or international recognition in their field. A sustained record of effective teaching and mentorship of undergraduate or graduate students. A sustained record of external research support and leadership of an independent research program. Accomplishments sufficient to meet UTEP's requirements for appointment at the rank of Professor with tenure. Preference will be given to candidates who demonstrate one or more of the following: Evidence of success in, or strong potential for, securing competitive external research funding. A record of high-quality peer-reviewed publications appropriate to the applicant's career stage. Evidence of effective and innovative teaching at the undergraduate or graduate level. A strong record of mentoring undergraduate or graduate researchers. Experience supporting the academic and professional success of students from a broad range of educational and personal backgrounds, including first-generation college students. The potential to contribute to the continued growth of UTEP's undergraduate and graduate programs in communication. Preference will be given to candidates who have teaching and/or research expertise in one or more areas listed in the job description. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants must electronically submit: (1) letter of interest that specifies teaching areas and research expertise; (2) curriculum vita; (3) one scholarly publication (4) recent evidence of teaching effectiveness; (5) names and full contact information of three references that we may contact. Recommendation letters are needed for candidates who are invited for campus visits for further consideration. If you have questions about the position, please contact the Job Search Committee Chair, Dr. Carlos Tarin, catarin@utep.edu . Application Review Date: Review of applications will begin October 18, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204454": {
      "title": "Open Rank Tenured or Tenure-Track Faculty in Public and Nonprofit Mgmt",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Open Rank Tenured or Tenure-Track Faculty in Public and Nonprofit Mgmt - Public Administration The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Open Rank Tenured or Tenure-Track Faculty in Public and Nonprofit Mgmt - Public Administration Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Open Rank Tenured or Tenure-Track Faculty in Public and Nonprofit Mgmt - Public Administration Category: Liberal Arts Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Political Science and Public Administration Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 3, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-CLA-09 Position ID: 204454 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. Description of Department/College Job Description: With nearly 7,000 students and more than 20 departments and programs, the College of Liberal Arts is the largest college at UTEP. The college offers 36 bachelor's, 24 master's, and four Ph.D. degrees in the fine and performing arts, the humanities, and the social and behavioral sciences. The college also provides most of the UTEP core curriculum to students in all colleges. The college's undergraduate and graduate programs house many excellent faculty who have been recognized as national and international leaders in their fields. Faculty conduct interdisciplinary research in Latinx culture, health disparities, social justice, border studies, migration, environmental communication, and numerous other fields that enrich our bicultural and binational region. Under the UTEP EDGE, faculty and students across the college engage in a variety of high-impact practices including community engagement and research on both sides of the border, study abroad/away, internships, and capstone experiences that help prepare students for lifelong success. The Department of Political Science and Public Administration at UTEP is seeking to fill one open-rank tenured or tenure-track position beginning Fall 2027. We encourage applications from scholars advancing research on public and nonprofit management broadly, whose research impacts the core public administration literature. The successful candidate will teach in the Master of Public Administration and the newly launched B.A. in Public Administration. The ability to teach MPA and undergraduate courses is required. The ability to teach courses in quantitative research methods (graduate and undergraduate) is desirable. The teaching load is 2-2. We prefer candidates who can engage with the local public and/or nonprofit community through community-engaged teaching and applied research. The Department's MPA degree is accredited by the Network of Schools of Public Policy, Affairs and Administration (NASPAA). We also offer a Graduate Certificate in Nonprofit Administration, and an undergraduate concentration and minor in Public Administration. Requirements: Required Qualifications: Applicants must have a Ph.D. in Public Administration or a closely related field from an accredited university. ABD applicants will be considered if a doctorate will be in hand by Fall 2027. An active and promising research agenda and published scholarly journal articles are required. A strong record of peer-reviewed publications will ultimately be required for tenure. The potential to secure external funding is highly valued. Preferred Areas: Preference will be given to candidates who have a demonstrated research agenda and teaching expertise in public and/or nonprofit management, including, local government, grant writing and fund development for nonprofits, urban planning/administration, public participation, or emergency management. We are especially interested in candidates who can contribute to our involvement with local governments in our region, as well as with the Texas City Management Association (TCMA) and the International City/County Management Association (ICMA). Demonstrated experience or potential for teaching and mentoring students at a Hispanic-Serving Institution or with underserved student populations is also preferred. Rank Specification Qualifications Associate Professor: Candidates must demonstrate sustained excellence across research, teaching, and service, including a strong record of publication in leading peer-reviewed journals, demonstrated effectiveness in teaching and course development, and meaningful leadership and service contributions to the department, university, and profession. Candidates with a successful record of securing and managing external funding or establishing applied research partnerships are strongly preferred. Involvement and familiarity with NASPAA are also preferred. Full Professor: Candidates must demonstrate sustained scholarly excellence, including a substantial record of impactful peer-reviewed publications and a well-established national or international reputation. Candidates must also have a sustained record of teaching excellence, effective mentorship of students and junior faculty, and significant leadership and service. A demonstrated record of securing and managing external research funding is preferred. Involvement and familiarity with NASPAA are also preferred. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants must electronically submit: (1) letter of interest that outlines your research agenda (including research trajectory and for senior positions, trajectory and impact) and teaching expertise; (2) curriculum vita; (3) one scholarly publication or working paper; (4) evidence of teaching effectiveness; and (5) names and full contact information of three references that we may contact. If you have questions about the position, please contact the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Eric Boyer at ejboyer@utep.edu Review of applications will begin on November 15, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=197844": {
      "title": "Professor or Associate Professor of Open-Source Intelligence",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Professor or Associate Professor of Open-Source Intelligence The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Professor or Associate Professor of Open-Source Intelligence Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Professor or Associate Professor of Open-Source Intelligence Category: Liberal Arts Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Criminal Justice Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Mar 9, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 26-CLA-14 Position ID: 197844 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: UTEP is a Carnegie R1 Doctoral University and a Carnegie Community Engaged university located in El Paso, Texas, a bicultural community of more than 800,000 people, in the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region. UTEP faculty are recognized nationally for their commitment to research, teaching, and student success. UTEP is the nation's leading Hispanic-serving institution and enrolls more than 25,000 students, 85% of whom are Hispanic and over half are first-generation. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. El Paso's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso is also an ideal location for year-round hiking, mountain biking, rock climbing and other outdoor activities in the nearby Franklin Mountains. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at Sun Bowl Stadium and the Don Haskins Center. Southwest University Park is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team. About the Department: The Department of Criminal Justice and Security Studies is one of the largest departments in the College of Liberal Arts, with six programs and 1,200 students. It offers an online B.A. in Security Studies (BASS), a Master of Science in Intelligence and National Security (INSS), and a Master of Defense and Strategic Studies (MDSS), as well as certificates in INSS and Open-Source Intelligence. The department also offers a B.A. in Criminal Justice, an equivalent 100 percent online B.A., and a Master of Science in Criminology and Criminal Justice. Job Description: The Department of Criminal Justice & Security Studies at the University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is seeking to fill a faculty position for a new open-source intelligence (OSINT) laboratory, created to be the first of its kind to study the complex security issues within the U.S.-Mexico border region. The faculty member will serve as the lead researcher of the Open-Source Lab and help lead efforts to establish the nation's premier institution analyzing U.S. Mexican border security, including how it is impacted by political, social, physical, and economic forces across (and beyond) the Western Hemisphere. The laboratory will use cutting edge OSINT techniques, build databases, help create scholarly research and analytical reports, and assist students in developing career relevant skills. This will be a full-time, tenured position beginning in Fall 2026. The faculty member will hold the rank of associate professor or professor. Responsibilities include helping to develop and lead the laboratory, analyzing data collected by the lab, obtaining external funding, and publishing scholarly research. The faculty member will teach courses and mentor students in open-source intelligence analysis. They will work closely with community partners across the university and in the public and private sectors in support of the laboratory and the Department. Requirements: Required Qualifications: Ph.D. from an accredited university in security studies, political science, public policy, or a closely related discipline to intelligence studies. Experience leading complex projects. Success in securing extramural funding and/or the ability to leverage government funds to sustain large-scale projects. Understanding of modern intelligence technology and tools. Preferred Areas: We are seeking candidates who have demonstrated research expertise in one or more of the following areas: open-source intelligence; intelligence collection and management; intelligence analysis; national security Professional experience in these areas within the intelligence and national security fields is welcomed. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: If you have questions about the position, please contact the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Charles Boehmer at crboehmer@utep.edu. Applicants must electronically submit: a letter of interest that includes leadership experience and research expertise, curriculum vita (please use APA format when listing publications), one scholarly publication, and names and full contact information of three references that we may contact. Application Review Date: The review of applications will begin March 31, 2026, and will continue until the position is filled. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=197977": {
      "title": "Assistant Research Professor in the Institute for Applied AI Innovation",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant Research Professor in the Institute for Applied AI Innovation The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Research Professor in the Institute for Applied AI Innovation Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Research Professor in the Institute for Applied AI Innovation Category: Office of Research and Innovation Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Institute for Applied AI Innovation Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Mar 11, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 26-RI-05 Position ID: 197977 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, opportunity, and community engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 25,000 students, 85% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 171 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The university has experienced significant growth in the last decade, achieving $158 Million in research expenditures in 2025.  UTEP ranks fourth among public, non-medical research institutions in Texas for total federal research spending, third in the state for research expenditures per tenured and tenure-track faculty and is in the top 5% nationally for research productivity metrics. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no income tax. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate the El Paso Chihuahuas and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. ABOUT THE INSTITUTE FOR APPLIED AI INNOVATION (AAII) AAII is UTEP's hub for interdisciplinary applied AI research, education, and workforce development. The Institute focuses on developing and deploying AI solutions that deliver real-world impact across key application domains, including health, education and workforce development, border logistics and infrastructure, cybersecurity and secure AI systems, economic development and innovation, and energy and water sustainability. AAII supports faculty and students through collaborative research projects, training programs, and partnerships with industry, government, and community organizations to advance regional, national, and global priorities. Job Description: UTEP invites applications for Assistant Research Professor positions within the Institute for Applied AI Innovation (AAII). These positions are funded through the UT System Regents' Research Excellence Program (RREP), a prestigious system-wide initiative established to elevate the national research standing of UT System's Emerging Research Universities. RREP aims to recruit promising research faculty of exceptional potential who demonstrate the capacity for nationally competitive extramural funding, graduate student mentorship, and transformative, transdisciplinary impact. We welcome applications from scholars across all academic disciplines whose work applies AI methods to challenges that intersect with AAII's mission. The successful candidate is expected to contribute to excellent research outcomes and to AAII's applied AI mission in one or more of the following areas: AI Models and Algorithms AI in Education and Workforce Development Border Logistics, Smart Infrastructure, and Supply Chain Systems Defense, Cybersecurity, and Secure AI Systems Economic Development, Innovation, and Entrepreneurship Energy, Water, and Sustainability Solutions Successful applicants will demonstrate the ability to conduct externally funded research, collaborate with interdisciplinary teams, publish scholarly work at reputable venues, mentor students and research staff, and contribute to scalable applied AI solutions. Requirements: Doctorate degree in one of the domains aligned with the AAII research areas, and a demonstrated expertise in the application of AI in the domain research area Strong communication, collaboration, and interdisciplinary teamwork skills Evidence of research productivity and potential to support externally funded projects Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin immediately, and applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Applicants must submit the following items: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research statement (max 3 pages) Names and contact information of at least three professional references For questions, contact Dr. Salamah Salamah, Search Committee Chair, at isalamah@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
