    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=197847": {
      "title": "Open Rank Research Professor for the Institute for Health and Lifespan Research (4 Positions)",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Open Rank Research Professor for the Institute for Health and Lifespan Research (4 Positions) The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Open Rank Research Professor for the Institute for Health and Lifespan Research (4 Positions) Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Open Rank Research Professor for the Institute for Health and Lifespan Research (4 Positions) Category: Office of Research and Innovation Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Institute for Health and Lifespan Research Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Mar 9, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 26-RI-02 Position ID: 197847 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, opportunity, and community engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 25,000 students, 85% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 171 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no income tax. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate the El Paso Chihuahuas and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. DESCRIPTION OF THE DEPARTMENT The UTEP Institute for Health and Lifespan Research (IHLR)'s vision is \"Healthier aging and lifespan for all by advancing integrative team science, training, and service.\" The IHLR's mission is to help current and future generations thrive across the lifespan through research, education, outreach, and service delivery locally, nationally and globally. The IHLR is a university-wide hub for advancing interdisciplinary team science in health and lifespan research. Its two central cores - One Health and Precision Health - provide a holistic framework to address health disparities across human, animal, plant, and environmental domains. Faculty hires of IHLR will collaborate across colleges and disciplines to pursue high-impact research, secure external funding, and translate discoveries into practice. The IHLR will be the home department of the three IHLR research faculty hired. Job Description: The University of Texas at El Paso invites applications for three strategic research faculty positions to anchor the newly established Institute for Health and Lifespan Research (IHLR). These positions include: Research Full Professor Research Associate Professor Research Assistant Professor The IHLR faculty are expected to advance interdisciplinary team science within the Institute's One Health and Precision Health cores, contributing to research that promotes healthier aging and improved lifespan across human, animal, plant, and environmental domains. Candidates with expertise relevant to lifespan health, regional health disparities, and integrative biological-behavioral-environmental approaches, including areas such as brain and behavioral health, metabolic and cardiovascular health, cancer, infectious diseases, and environmental determinants of health, are especially encouraged to apply. Successful candidates will pursue high-impact, collaborative research that secures external funding and translates discoveries into practice through implementation science, health services research, behavioral intervention science, and/or  translational research focused on health disparities, The IHLR faculty are expected to accelerate research in health and lifespan research through team & convergence science expertise/interest while actively engaging in interdisciplinary and translational research teams. Preference will be given to candidates with expertise and prior research success evidence by grants (federal, state, private) and scholarly products in IHLR priority areas of health and lifespan research, particularly in the areas of brain and behavioral health, healthy aging, and regional health disparities of focus (diabetes, cardiovascular health, cancer). IHLR faculty will contribute to UTEP's interdisciplinary mission through research, teaching, and service. Candidates are expected to foster community, industry and government partnerships to advance IHLR goals. Successful candidates will engage in high-impact, collaborative research addressing pressing health challenges within and/or across human, animal, plant, and environmental domains. Areas of expertise may include public health, microbiology, immunology, pharmacy, biomedical engineering, epigenetics, genomics, systems level approaches. implementation science, anthropology, infectious diseases, and related disciplines/fields. Requirements: Research Full Professor Earned doctorate (PHD, ScD, MD, DrPH, DMD etc. or equivalent) in a in a health-related discipline, biomedical sciences, engineering, social sciences, or other relevant field. Existing research program with sustained external funding as PI in research areas relevant to IHLR goals and cores. Strong record of publications in high-impact journals. Experience leading interdisciplinary projects or multi-institutional collaborations. Demonstrated ability to mentor graduate students and junior researchers. Evidence of leadership in professional organizations or research networks. Research Associate Professor Earned doctorate (PHD, ScD, MD, DrPH, DMD etc. or equivalent) in a health-related discipline, biomedical sciences, engineering, social sciences, or other relevant field. Demonstrated research productivity with publications in peer-reviewed journals. Evidence of external funding or strong potential for securing competitive grants as PI or co-PI in research areas relevant to IHLR goals and cores. Experience contributing to interdisciplinary research teams. Ability to supervise graduate students and collaborate across disciplines. Research Assistant Professor Earned doctorate (PHD, ScD, MD, DrPH, DMD etc. or equivalent) in a health-related discipline, biomedical sciences, engineering, social sciences, or other relevant field. Emerging research agenda with publications in peer-reviewed journals. Clear potential for securing external funding and contributing to team science. Interest in interdisciplinary research and team science aligned with IHLR's mission. Commitment to mentoring students and engaging in collaborative scholarship. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS Experience with large-scale federally funded projects or multi-million-dollar grants. Expertise in One Health, Precision Health, or lifespan research frameworks. Ability to integrate AI, data science, or advanced analytics into health research. Experience in community-engaged research or working with underserved/rural populations. Bilingual ability (English/Spanish) for research in the U.S.-Mexico border region. Record of collaboration with national labs, industry, and/or government agencies. Global health research experience Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin immediately and applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Please clearly indicate in your letter of interest which rank and position you want to be considered for. Required documents that candidates must submit: (1) a cover letter, 2) curriculum vitae, 3) Research philosophy and 3-year strategic plan for research at the IHLR with specific, time-bound expected outcomes and, 4) contact information for at least three professional references. Questions should be directed to the Search Committee Chair - Dr. Thenral Mangadu, Director, IHLR; Associate Vice President for Health and Interdisciplinary Research ( tdmangadu@utep.edu ) Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203704": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor in Physics",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor in Physics The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor in Physics Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor in Physics Category: Science Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Physics Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Aug 13, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-COS-08 Position ID: 203704 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. The Department of Physics at UTEP has rapidly growing research programs in experimental, computational, and theoretical physics. The research areas of the physics faculty include chemical and material physics, quantum physics, atmospheric physics, spectroscopy, astrophysics, and biophysics. The department has access to state-of-the-art experimental facilities for optical, microscopic, magnetic, and structural materials characterizations, as well as outstanding computational capabilities. Current faculty members specializing in astrophysics work on cosmic-ray transport, computational astrochemistry, and the star and planet formation process. The region surrounding El Paso has a favorable combination of clear nights and dark skies and is consequently home to several of the nation's top observing facilities, with McDonald Observatory, Apache Point, and the VLA all located within 4 hours drive from the campus. Job Description: The Department of Physics at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is seeking applications for a tenure track position in observational astronomy. The position is open to all areas of observational astronomy, with preference towards research in interstellar medium, dust evolution, and the star and planet formation process. The successful candidate should be able to establish and maintain an independent research program at UTEP that complements existing research in the Department, involve undergraduate and graduate students in research, teach core physics and astronomy courses at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, perform service to the Department, College, and University, and engage in outreach activities. The new hire will be a core faculty member of the Physics Graduate Program. Requirements: Candidates must have a PhD in physics, astronomy, or a related field; a strong record of research accomplishments; strong verbal and written communication skills; and demonstrated or clear potential for effective teaching of core physics courses at both the undergraduate and graduate levels. Candidates with postdoctoral experience will be given preference. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Candidates must submit: 1) letter of interest, 2) curriculum vitae that includes a list of publications, 3) research plan, 4) description of teaching philosophy, and 5) contact information for three references. Review of applications will begin November 15th and continue until the position is filled. Applicants can reach out to the search committee chair, Dr. S. Singamaneni (srao@utep.edu), if they have any questions. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204603": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Earth, Environmental and Resources Sciences",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Earth, Environmental and Resources Sciences The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant Professor of Earth, Environmental and Resources Sciences Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant Professor of Earth, Environmental and Resources Sciences Category: Science Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Earth, Environmental and Resources Sciences Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 9, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-COS(m)-09 Position ID: 204603 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. Job Description: The Department of Earth, Environmental, and Resource Sciences at UTEP is a vibrant group of 20 faculty representing a wide variety of geology, geophysics, hydrology, environmental science, and geography expertise. The degrees the Department offers include the B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. in Geological Sciences, the B.S. and M.S. in Environmental Science, the M.S. in Geophysics, and a minor in Geography. Our Ph.D. in Geological Sciences includes students with interests in any of these subdisciplines. We also support student training in cross-campus interdisciplinary Ph.D. programs including the Environmental Science and Engineering, Computational Science, and Data Science Ph.D. programs and collaborate frequently with the new Mining Engineering program and the Metallurgical and Materials Engineering program of the College of Engineering. The Department research portfolio addresses local to global challenges in the environmental and natural resources fields while taking advantage of our regional geographic setting for research and education. Our integrative and collaborative efforts have resulted in research expenditures of $2.5M per year. The department has a long history in the study of earth and mineral resources and considers professional and entrepreneurial geoscience a research theme ( https://www.utep.edu/science/geology/research-themes/professional-geoscience.html ). The Department of Earth, Environmental and Resource Sciences occupies an attractive, 90,000-sq. ft. building containing offices, laboratories, and classrooms. We have mineral separation equipment, a multi-collector ICP-MS, laser ablation ICP-MS, an IRMS, variable-pressure SEM-EDS, scanning micro-XRF and bench-top XRD systems, and a Malvern particle size analyzer. The Department has a research computing lab with high-end workstations. Geophysical research infrastructure includes multiple surface and downhole geophysical tools and extensive computational and software resources. More information about the activities and facilities in the Department can be found at https://www.utep.edu/science/geology/ . In addition, the collaborative research environment at UTEP affords access to a variety of analytical equipment in the Departments of Metallurgy and Materials Science, Chemistry, and Biological Sciences and the Nanomaterials Integration Lab. Interactions with the Center for Environmental Resource Management and other multidisciplinary research centers are encouraged. A new building dedicated to interdisciplinary research was completed in 2020. The Department of Earth, Environmental and Resource Sciences invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor position in Subsurface Geology, beginning August 2027. We are seeking highly collaborative applicants with expertise in the exploration, production, or storage of subsurface resources such as geothermal fluids, groundwater/brine, minerals, carbon dioxide, and hydrogen. Specific areas of expertise may include but not limit to subsurface characterization of rock properties (such as porosity, permeability, fracture distribution, reactivity and geometry), geologic modeling/reservoir simulation, fluid-rock interactions, fluid injection and extraction in porous and permeable rock formations. Applicants should enhance the core strengths of our department in areas such as geophysics, geochemistry, human-environment interactions, economic geology, land use/land cover change, hydrology, field geology, tectonics, and planetary science. Candidates are encouraged to develop contacts and partnerships with industry and organizations involved in the energy transition and mineral resource development. The successful candidate will be expected to attract research funding from external sources. Teaching may include an introductory course in Earth Science or Environmental Science and upper division and/or graduate classes in topics of their choice agreed upon with the Department Chair. In addition to advising M.S. and Ph.D. students from the Department of Earth, Environmental, and Resource Sciences, the selected candidate will have the opportunity to participate in the interdisciplinary Environmental Science undergraduate and doctoral programs and the new undergraduate program in Mining Engineering (starting fall 2027). Requirements: The Department of Earth, Environmental and Resource Sciences is hiring a faculty member in the broadly-defined area of subsurface geology who is capable of teaching at the undergraduate and graduate levels, conducting and establishing an extramurally funded and active research portfolio, publishing in peer-reviewed journals, and mentoring students at all levels. A Ph.D. in Geological Sciences or a closely related field at the time of appointment is required. Preferred Qualifiactions: Candidates will have postdoctoral research experience, track record of peer-reviewed publications, previously secured research funding, collaborations and/or experience working with industry, and teaching experience is desirable. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin on November 15, 2026 and applications will continue to be reviewed until the position is filled. Applications must submit the following: 1) cover letter of interest; 2) curriculum vitae; 3) statement of research interests; 4) statement of teaching philosophy and interests; 5) description of how the applicant would enhance broader impacts in the geosciences and our communities; and 6) complete contact information for at least three references. Questions should be directed to Chair of the Search Committee Dr. Lin Ma at lma@utep.edu or (915) 747-5218. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205033": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor in Mathematical Sciences",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor in Mathematical Sciences The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor in Mathematical Sciences Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant/Associate Professor in Mathematical Sciences Category: Science Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Mathematical Sciences Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 21, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-COS-05 Position ID: 205033 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. Job Description: The Department of Mathematical Sciences consists of 28 tenured and tenure-track faculty members with strong expertise in the areas of algebra and combinatorics, fixed point theory, statistics, bioinformatics, computational science, topology, data science applied mathematics and mathematics/statistics education. Notable accomplishments of the productive faculty include publication of high-impact peer-reviewed manuscripts and annual research expenditures of $1.2M. A major addition to Department's programs is the Data Science PhD program which launched in 2021 and has already produced graduates. The Department also contributes to interdisciplinary programs across the University including the Bioinformatics and Computational Science graduate programs in the College of Science. More information is available at the Department of Mathematical Sciences' website: https://www.utep.edu/science/math/ . The Department of Mathematical Sciences at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is seeking a mathematical scientist with expertise and experience in image analysis for a tenure track assistant/associate professor position. Successful candidates are expected to develop productive research programs, mentor and teach at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, and to direct M.S. theses and Ph.D. dissertations. Candidates with experience in industry and/or applied interdisciplinary research projects are encouraged to apply. Anticipated start date is fall 2027. Requirements: Applicants must have a Ph.D. in a mathematical, computational, or data science related field, postdoctoral experience, a strong record of research accomplishments, and the capacity to secure extramural funding. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin immediately and applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Candidates must submit: 1) letter of interest, 2) curriculum vitae, 3) statement of research interests, 4) brief outline of teaching philosophy, 5) copies of up to three relevant authored or co-authored publications, and 6) complete contact information for at least three references. Hiring decisions are subject to budget approval. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204252": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate Professor of Organic Chemistry",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor of Organic Chemistry The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant/Associate Professor of Organic Chemistry Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant/Associate Professor of Organic Chemistry Category: Science Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Chemistry and Biochemistry Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Aug 28, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-COS-03 Position ID: 204252 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) offers B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. programs and maintains active research programs in organic chemistry, inorganic chemistry, biochemistry, materials science, analytical chemistry, environmental chemistry, medicinal chemistry and related disciplines. Faculty are committed to excellence in teaching, research, and mentoring while preparing students for careers in academia, industry, government laboratories, and the health professions. The UTEP College of Science is a research-intensive college that includes the Departments of Biological Sciences, Chemistry and Biochemistry, Earth, Environmental and Resource Sciences, Mathematical Sciences, and Physics. The College promotes excellence in education, research, and community engagement while providing students with opportunities for hands-on research and interdisciplinary collaboration. As part of an R1 university, the College prepares students for leadership and professional success in science, healthcare, industry, and public service. Job Description: The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry at UTEP invites applications for a faculty position in Organic Chemistry at the Assistant or Associate Professor level. The successful candidate is expected to establish and maintain an innovative, externally funded research program in organic chemistry, contribute to interdisciplinary research collaborations, and mentor and train undergraduate, M.S., and Ph.D. students. The faculty member will teach undergraduate and graduate courses in organic chemistry and participate in service activities that support the missions of the department, college, and university. Candidates with expertise in any area of organic chemistry are encouraged to apply. Preference will be given to candidates whose research programs involve hands-on experimental studies in organic chemistry. Areas of interest include, but are not limited to, synthetic organic chemistry, mechanistic organic chemistry, chemical biology, medicinal chemistry, catalysis, organometallic chemistry, materials chemistry, and related areas. The successful candidate will contribute to UTEP's mission through excellence in research, education, student success, and community engagement. Requirements: Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Chemistry or a closely related field. Postdoctoral research experience or equivalent in organic chemistry or a closely related field. Demonstrated research productivity in organic chemistry, as evidenced by peer-reviewed publications. Preferred Qualifications: Research program involving hands-on experimental studies in organic chemistry. Research interests that complement and expand existing departmental strengths in organic chemistry. Research program that leverages and enhances the Department's shared instrumentation and core facilities, including NMR spectroscopy, mass spectrometry, X-ray crystallography, and related research infrastructure. Interest in interdisciplinary research collaborations within chemistry and related STEM disciplines. Experience mentoring undergraduate and/or graduate student researchers. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Application materials must include: 1.  Cover Letter. Applicants should indicate in their cover letter whether they wish to be considered for appointment at the Assistant Professor or Associate Professor rank. 2.  Curriculum Vitae. 3.  Research Statement describing prior research accomplishments and future plans for establishing and maintaining an independent, externally funded research program. 4.  Teaching and Mentoring Statement describing teaching philosophy, mentoring experience, and approaches to student success. 5.  Contact information for at least three professional references. Questions regarding the position may be directed to Dr. Katja Michael, Search Committee Chair, at kmichael@utep.edu. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204341": {
      "title": "Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Biochemistry",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Biochemistry The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Biochemistry Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Assistant/Associate/Full Professor of Biochemistry Category: Science Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Chemistry and Biochemistry Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 1, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-COS-04 Position ID: 204341 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. Job Description: College of Science UTEP's College of Science brings together five academic departments: Biological Sciences; Chemistry and Biochemistry; Earth, Environmental and Resource Sciences; Mathematical Sciences; and Physics. Research and education within the College extend from fundamental scientific discovery to applied work addressing challenges in human health, energy, materials, the environment, and data-intensive science. Undergraduate and graduate students are encouraged to participate directly in research and to collaborate across traditional disciplinary boundaries. As part of an R1 university, the College provides a setting in which faculty members pursue nationally and internationally significant scholarship while preparing students for careers in research, healthcare, education, industry, government, and public service. The College also advances UTEP's broader mission through scientific outreach, community engagement, and partnerships within and beyond the border region. Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry offers B.S., M.S., and Ph.D. degrees and supports a broad portfolio of research in chemistry, biochemistry, and related fields. Areas of faculty expertise include analytical, environmental, inorganic, medicinal, materials, and organic chemistry, as well as biochemistry and interdisciplinary research at the interfaces of these fields. Department faculty value high-quality instruction, innovative research, and close mentorship at every stage of a student's education. Students receive preparation for a wide range of professional paths, including academic and industrial research, government and national laboratories, healthcare, and the health professions. The Department's location within a highly collaborative College of Science creates additional opportunities for interdisciplinary training and research. Assistant, Associate, or Full Professor. The anticipated appointment date is Fall 2027. Rank and tenure status will be determined based on the successful candidate's qualifications and experience. The Department seeks a biochemist whose research focuses on post-translational modifications (PTMs) or a closely related area. Relevant research may include, but is not limited to, protein phosphorylation, glycosylation, acetylation, methylation, ubiquitination, or other chemical and enzymatic modifications that regulate protein function, stability, localization, signaling, and cellular physiology. Candidates whose work incorporates proteomics, mass spectrometry, chemical biology, structural biochemistry, molecular and cellular biochemistry, or complementary emerging approaches are particularly encouraged to apply. The successful candidate will establish and maintain an innovative, externally funded research program; publish significant scholarly work; teach undergraduate and graduate courses in biochemistry; mentor undergraduate, master's, and doctoral students; and contribute service to the Department, College, University, profession, and community. Teaching responsibilities may include the Department's core Biochemistry I and Biochemistry II courses, as well as advanced undergraduate and graduate courses related to the candidate's expertise. The successful candidate will also have opportunities to develop specialized courses in areas such as post-translational modifications, proteomics, chemical biology, and contemporary biochemical methods. The new faculty member will join a growing biochemistry program characterized by strong student demand and opportunities for collaboration in biochemistry, chemical biology, structural biology, molecular and cellular biology, biomedical research, and related fields. The position is expected to broaden the Department's research portfolio, expand its biochemistry curriculum, strengthen graduate education and research mentorship, and contribute to interdisciplinary collaborations across UTEP. This faculty appointment will support UTEP's mission of advancing discovery, increasing student participation in research, strengthening collaborative partnerships, and preparing a diverse scientific workforce to address regional, national, and global challenges. Requirements: All applicants must have: A Ph.D. or equivalent terminal degree in biochemistry, chemistry, chemical biology, molecular biology, or a closely related discipline by the anticipated start date. A record of research accomplishments in biochemistry or a closely related field appropriate to the applicant's career stage. The ability to develop and sustain an independent, externally funded research program. The ability to teach biochemistry effectively at the undergraduate and graduate levels. The ability and commitment to mentor undergraduate and graduate students in coursework and research. The ability to collaborate effectively with faculty and students across academic disciplines and backgrounds. Effective oral and written communication skills. For appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor, applicants must demonstrate: Evidence of the potential to establish a productive, independent, and externally funded research program. Evidence of the potential for effective undergraduate and graduate teaching and student mentorship. For appointment at the rank of Associate Professor, applicants must also demonstrate: A sustained record of significant scholarly achievement and peer-reviewed publication. A record of effective undergraduate or graduate teaching and student mentorship. A record of obtaining external research support. Accomplishments sufficient to meet UTEP's requirements for appointment at the rank of Associate Professor with tenure. For appointment at the rank of Professor, applicants must also demonstrate: A sustained and distinguished record of scholarly achievement and national or international recognition in their field. A sustained record of effective teaching and mentorship of undergraduate or graduate students. A sustained record of external research support and leadership of an independent research program. Accomplishments sufficient to meet UTEP's requirements for appointment at the rank of Professor with tenure. Preference will be given to candidates who demonstrate one or more of the following: Research expertise in post-translational modifications, including phosphorylation, glycosylation, acetylation, methylation, ubiquitination, or related mechanisms that regulate protein function and cellular processes. Expertise in proteomics, mass spectrometry, chemical biology, molecular and cellular biochemistry, structural biochemistry, or other modern biochemical and analytical methods. A research program addressing important questions related to human health or disease, including cancer, neurodegenerative disorders, metabolic diseases, immune regulation, or other areas of fundamental or translational biomedical significance. A record of, or strong potential for, collaboration with UTEP researchers in biochemistry, chemistry, biological sciences, biomedical sciences, bioengineering, pharmacy, materials science, data science, or other health-related disciplines. Postdoctoral research experience or equivalent advanced professional research experience. Evidence of success in, or strong potential for, securing competitive external research funding. A record of high-quality peer-reviewed publications appropriate to the applicant's career stage. Evidence of effective and innovative teaching at the undergraduate or graduate level. Experience developing advanced courses, laboratory modules, or research-based learning activities in biochemistry or related fields. A strong record of mentoring undergraduate or graduate researchers. Experience supporting the academic and professional success of students from a broad range of educational and personal backgrounds, including first-generation college students. The potential to contribute to the continued growth of UTEP's undergraduate and graduate programs in biochemistry. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Application materials must include: Cover letter. Applicants should indicate whether they wish to be considered for appointment at the rank of Assistant Professor, Associate Professor, Full Professor, or more than one rank. Curriculum vitae. Research statement describing the applicant's prior research accomplishments and future plans for establishing and sustaining an independent, externally funded research program. Teaching and mentoring statement describing the applicant's teaching philosophy, teaching and mentoring experience, and approaches to promoting student learning and success. Professional references. Applicants must provide the names and contact information of at least three professional references. Candidates applying at the Associate Professor or Professor rank may also be asked to provide additional references, as required, during the final stage of the appointment process. Evaluation of applications will begin upon receipt and will continue until the position is filled. Questions regarding the position may be directed to Dr. Chuan (River) Xiao, Chair of the Search Committee, at cxiao@utep.edu. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204946": {
      "title": "Chair of Physics Department",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Chair of Physics Department The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Chair of Physics Department Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Chair of Physics Department Category: Science Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Physics Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Sep 17, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Position ID: 204946 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. Job Description: The Department of Physics at UTEP has rapidly growing research programs in experimental, computational, and theoretical physics with 24 faculty members, 5 postdoctoral researchers, 4 research assistant professors, and 3 lecturers. The research areas of the physics faculty include chemical physics, magnetism, energy storage, quantum physics, quantum computation and quantum information, quantum optics, density functional theory, atmospheric physics, physics of materials in extreme environment, optical spectroscopy & microscopy, functional materials, nuclear theory, astrophysics, nano- and biomaterials, and experimental and computational biophysics. The department has access to state-of-the-art experimental facilities for optical, microscopic, magnetic, and structural characterizations of materials, as well as outstanding computational capabilities. The department faculty has access to facilities at several national laboratories, for example, Pacific Northwest National Laboratory, Oak Ridge National Laboratory, Lawrence Berkeley National Lab, Lawrence Livermore National Lab and Los Alamos National Laboratory, through active collaborations or user-proposal routes. The Department offers B.S. in Physics, M.S. in Physics, and Ph.D. in Physics with an emphasis on quantum science. Our faculty also contribute to other interdisciplinary Ph.D. programs at UTEP in Materials Science and Engineering, Environmental Science and Engineering, Computational Science, Data Science, and Biomedical Engineering. The department is committed to student success, preparing the next generation physicists with knowledge, skills and visions. The Department of Physics has initiated an annual seminar series at UTEP in recognition of the International Day for Women and Girls in Science. Efforts like this have exemplified UTEP's broader commitment to excellence through inclusivity by offering other students from El Paso's community as well as from developing nations an opportunity to excel in physics. The College of Science at The University of Texas at El Paso is seeking a dynamic leader to serve as Chair of the Physics Department. The Chair will join a multidisciplinary, collaborative, growing department with research activities spanning diverse areas of astrophysics, atmospheric physics, biophysics, condensed matter physics, magnetism, molecular physics, nuclear theory, optical spectroscopy, and quantum information science with broad applications in energy storage, functional materials, nano- and bio-materials. The successful candidate is expected to be an active researcher with a vision for developing research initiatives and strengthening collaborations and partnerships, and a strong leader with program, interpersonal, and personnel management skills. The successful candidate will demonstrate a commitment to undergraduate and graduate student success serving UTEP's mission of access and excellence as a premier Hispanic Serving Institution. Requirements: PhD in a discipline appropriate for the Department of Physics Current academic rank of tenured full professor (or equivalent rank) Demonstrated leadership skills Excellent record of program, people, time, and financial management Outstanding record of research, mentoring, and teaching Commitment to the core values of collaboration, innovation, and accessibility Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Applicants should submit (1) a curriculum vitae, (2) contact information for 5 references, and (3) a letter of interest addressing experience with, and contributions to research, education, administration, and leadership opportunities relevant to UTEP's mission.  Applications that are not complete will not be considered. Applicants are encouraged to apply by November 15, 2026. Review of applications will continue until the position is filled. Questions about this position should be directed to Search Committee Chair, Dr. Lixin Jin, ljin2@utep.edu . Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=205807": {
      "title": "Director",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Director - Border Biomedical Research Center (BBRC) The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Director - Border Biomedical Research Center (BBRC) Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Director - Border Biomedical Research Center (BBRC) Category: Science Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: College of Science Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Oct 14, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-COS-10 Position ID: 205807 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. Learn more about faculty life at UTEP and what it's like to live and work in El Paso: Life & Work in El Paso Job Description: UTEP's College of Science comprises five academic departments: Biological Sciences, Chemistry and Biochemistry, Earth, Environmental and Resource Sciences, Mathematical Sciences, and Physics. Research and education within the College extend from fundamental scientific discovery to applied work addressing challenges in human health, energy, materials, the environment, and data-intensive science. Undergraduate and graduate students are encouraged to participate directly in research and to collaborate across traditional disciplinary boundaries. As an R1 institution, the College provides a setting in which faculty members pursue nationally and internationally significant scholarship while preparing students for careers in research, healthcare, education, industry, government, and public service. The College also advances UTEP's broader mission through scientific outreach, community engagement, and partnerships within and beyond the border region. Position Overview UTEP invites applications and nominations for the position of Director of the Border Biomedical Research Center (BBRC).  The successful candidate will be appointed as a tenured Full Professor and will join the leadership team of UTEP's National Institutes of Health (NIH) U54 award, funded by the National Institute on Minority Health and Health Disparities (NIMHD) through the Research Centers in Minority Institutions (RCMI) Program. The BBRC is a hub for interdisciplinary biomedical research focused on Hispanic health disparities and priorities. The BBRC supports biomedical research across basic, behavioral, translational, implementation, and community-engaged domains; develops students and early-stage investigators; collaborates with community stakeholders and local health systems; and builds regional and national partnerships to address health disparities, disease mechanisms, and develop community-based interventions. The BBRC's accomplishments contributed to UTEP's attainment of the R1 Carnegie designation in 2019 and its reaffirmation in 2022. The BBRC seeks a visionary and nationally recognized biomedical scientist with a sustained record of NIH funding and funding from other agencies, demonstrated leadership of complex multidisciplinary research programs or centers, and a strong commitment to the missions of Minority-Serving and and/or Hispanic-Serving Institutions. The successful candidate will be appointed as a tenured Full Professor in a College of Science department consistent with their academic and research background and will report administratively to the Dean of the College of Science. Key Responsibilities Center Leadership, Growth, and Sustainability Provide strategic and scientific leadership for the BBRC, fostering a collaborative environment that advances basic biomedical, behavioral, population, clinical, translational and implementation research addressing health disparities and the health priorities of the US-Mexico border region. Provide strategic oversight of the BBRC's specialized research core facilities, ensuring that their capabilities and services effectively support investigators and advance the Center's scientific mission. Cultivate cross-college and interdisciplinary partnerships, multi-institutional collaborations, and strengthen partnerships with healthcare systems and community organizations with a focus on advancing the BBRC's research mission and regional impact. Execute a strategy for the BBRC's long-term financial and operational sustainability and growth through competitive grant renewals, new- center-level funding opportunities, diversified revenue sources, and institutional investment. Align the BBRC's research portfolio and cores capabilities with UTEP's institutional priorities, emerging scientific opportunities, and national priorities in health disparities and biomedical research. Investigator Mentoring and Development Mentor early-career faculty, research project leaders, and postdoctoral fellows in developing independent research programs and securing competitive peer-reviewed external funding, including NIH R01 or equivalent awards. Collaborate with the appropriate colleges and departments to identify and/or recruit faculty aligned with the BBRC's strategic mission. Resource Management and Administration Provide responsible stewardship of the BBRC's financial resources and oversee the strategic allocation of extramural funding, core-facility revenue, and institutional support in partnership with the College of Science and the UTEP Central Administration. Engage the BBRC's external advisory committee and lead its internal steering committee, using a comprehensive evaluation framework to assess Center performance, productivity and research impact. Lead the BBRC's governance structure, ensuring effective decision-making, accountability, compliance, and transparent communication with internal and external stakeholders. Requirements: Education: M.D., Ph.D., or equivalent degree. Academic Rank: Hold the rank of Full Professor with tenure or possess credentials appropriate for appointment at that rank in a department within the UTEP College of Science (e.g., Biological Sciences, Chemistry and Biochemistry, Mathematical Sciences). Research and Funding Track Record: Must have a distinguished record of independent, peer-reviewed NIH funding such as R01, U54, P20, or comparable research awards, and a strong record of influential biomedical scholarship. Programmatic Expertise: Demonstrated leadership experience in managing or directing NIH-funded centers and programs, with a comprehensive understanding of the research and capacity-building needs of Minority-Serving research universities. Leadership and Administrative Experience: Proven record of effective leadership in a research center, institute, academic department, or comparable unit; including oversight of complex budgets, personnel, operations, and large-scale project management. Commitment to Mentoring: Demonstrated commitment to mentoring students, postdoctoral fellows, and early-career faculty in the biomedical sciences. Preferred Qualifications Additional experience with educational/training awards is desired. Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Interested candidates should submit a curriculum vitae and a letter of interest integrating and detailing their: Research experience and a comprehensive history of current and past NIH funding; administrative philosophy, including a specific narrative on the role and importance of biomedical research centers within academic institutions; and their vision and approach to health disparities research. Include the names and contact information of four professional references who are familiar with the candidate's scientific achievements, leadership track record and administrative capabilities. Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=204272": {
      "title": "Endowed Welch Chair of Chemistry",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Endowed Welch Chair of Chemistry The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Endowed Welch Chair of Chemistry Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Endowed Welch Chair of Chemistry Category: Science Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Chemistry and Biochemistry Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Aug 31, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-COS-03 Position ID: 204272 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. The city's location on the Rio Grande in the Southwest where two countries and three states meet makes it an ideal venue for academic programs and research studies on topics of national interest, including bilingual education/language acquisition, health and health disparities, international relations, international trade, migration, and environmental sustainability and infrastructure. The University enjoys strategic partnerships with two Department of Energy national laboratories in the region, multiple national laboratories beyond the region, and sits on the edge of White Sands Missile Range, making engineering and scientific research both a priority and an opportunity. Along with its designation among the top 5% of universities in America for research, UTEP is also nationally recognized as an Opportunity University by the Carnegie Classification for its positive impact on economic mobility for students after graduation. In 2024, Forbes found that UTEP students graduate with the lowest average debt of any college or university in Texas. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. The city's active arts and culture community features the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, a nationally recognized chamber music festival, a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, museums, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. In 2024, El Paso opened La Nube, a unique hybrid institution that merges the educational aspects of a science center with the interactive experiences of a children's museum. UTEP adds to the arts and entertainment scene with concerts and live events at the city's largest indoor and outdoor venues; theater, music and dance productions; contemporary and student art galleries; and a natural history museum and desert gardens. El Paso residents can also easily travel to the sister city of Ciudad Juárez, Mexico, to enjoy great food, markets and cultural events. El Paso/Juárez is the fifth largest manufacturing region in North America and the center of commerce, education and health services for the region. A family-oriented community, the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable for a large metropolitan area with the average home value of $224,000 in 2025. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation.Texas has no state income tax and, when it comes to public safety, El Paso is consistently ranked among the safest large cities in the United States. El Paso is also an ideal location for people who love the outdoors. It is home to one of the nation's largest urban parks - Franklin Mountains State Park - and many options for year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing at Hueco Tanks State Park and nearby White Sands, Guadalupe Mountains and Carlsbad Caverns national parks. For sports fans, the UTEP Miners offer exciting Division I college athletics at several campus venues including Sun Bowl Stadium - where one of the oldest football bowl games takes place annually - and the Don Haskins Center arena. Southwest University Park, located a mile away in lively downtown El Paso, is home to the El Paso Chihuahuas, the Triple-A baseball affiliate of the San Diego Padres, and the El Paso Locomotive FC, a professional soccer team founded in 2019. Job Description: The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry at UTEP is a dynamic and research-active department housed in a state-of-the-art facility equipped with major instrumentation including NMR, X-ray diffraction (single crystal and powder), EPR, SQUID magnetometry, and XPS. The department offers ACS-certified B.S. degrees in Chemistry and Biochemistry, as well as M.S. and Ph.D. degrees. The Ph.D. program emphasizes high-impact research and strong mentorship, with a commitment to student success in STEM. The department invites nominations and applications for the endowed Welch Chair in Chemistry. This prestigious position was established through an endowment from the Welch Foundation and is intended for a nationally recognized leader in chemical research. The successful candidate will be expected to maintain an internationally visible research program, actively pursue external funding, and provide visionary leadership that enhances the department's research capacity and collaborative culture. We seek a candidate whose expertise complements or unifies existing strengths in the department, including energy, catalysis, molecular design, and chemical biology. The Chair is expected to contribute to the department's graduate and undergraduate education, mentor faculty and students across all levels, and play an active role in shaping the future direction and growth of the department. The successful candidate will also be well-positioned to lead and participate in center-scale, interdisciplinary funding initiatives aligned with state and federal research priorities. Requirements: Required Qualifications: A Ph.D. in Chemistry or a closely related field A record of nationally and internationally recognized research excellence Evidence of sustained extramural funding and scholarly productivity A commitment to mentoring students and early-career scientists Qualifications commensurate with appointment at the rank of full professor with tenure Preferred Qualifications: Experience leading interdisciplinary research initiatives Demonstrated success in mentoring junior faculty and building collaborative research environments Research interests that align with key departmental strengths in energy, catalysis, molecular design, or structural biochemistry Additional Information: In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position is filled. Applicants must submit the following: Cover letter Curriculum vitae Research statement Statement of teaching philosophy Contact information for at least three professional references For inquiries about the position, contact the Search Committee Chair, Dr. Skye Fortier, at asfortier@utep.edu Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
    },
    "https://utep.interviewexchange.com/jobofferdetails.jsp?JOBID=203866": {
      "title": "Open Rank Professor in Mathematical Sciences",
      "description": "The University of Texas at El Paso\nJob Posting: Open Rank Professor in Mathematical Sciences The University of Texas at El Paso \n\tJob Posting: Open Rank Professor in Mathematical Sciences Title Human Resources Subscribe to Email Alerts Returning Applicant Login Previous Next Careers > Search Jobs > Search Results > Job Details Open Rank Professor in Mathematical Sciences Category: Science Subscribe: New Job Alerts RSS Job Feed Department: Mathematical Sciences Locations: El Paso, TX Posted: Aug 18, 2026 Closes: Open Until Filled Type: Full-time Ref. No.: 27-COS-06 Position ID: 203866 Share About The University of Texas at El Paso: The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is a Carnegie R1, Opportunity, and Community-Engaged university at the heart of the U.S.-Mexico border region that is increasing access to excellent higher education. We advance discovery of public value and positively impact the health, culture, education, and economy of the community we serve. UTEP enrolls more than 26,000 students, 84% of whom are Hispanic and half of whom are the first in their families to attend college. UTEP offers 169 bachelor's, master's and doctoral degree programs at the only open-access, top-tier research university in America. Located at the westernmost tip of Texas, El Paso is set against the backdrop of the Franklin Mountains and the Chihuahuan Desert in one of the largest binational communities in the world. A family-oriented community that is one of the safest large cities in the U.S., the cost of living in El Paso is very reasonable with an average home value of $224,000 in 2025, and Texas has no state income tax. In 2026, U.S. News and World report named El Paso the best big city to live in the nation. The region's rich culture includes a vibrant street art and mural scene, art galleries, a new children's museum and science center, the state's longest-running symphony orchestra, and a full schedule of seasonal events and festivals. Sports fans will enjoy Division I college athletics, Triple-A baseball affiliate El Paso Chihuahuas, and professional soccer team El Paso Locomotive FC, and outdoor enthusiasts will be drawn to year-round hiking, mountain biking and rock climbing in nearby state and national parks. The Department of Mathematical Sciences consists of 28 tenured and tenure-track faculty members with strong expertise in the areas of algebra and combinatorics, fixed point theory, statistics, bioinformatics, computational science, topology, data science applied mathematics and mathematics/statistics education. Notable accomplishments of the productive faculty include publication of high-impact peer-reviewed manuscripts and annual research expenditures of $1.2M. A major addition to Department's programs is the Data Science PhD program which launched in 2021 and has already produced graduates. The Department also contributes to interdisciplinary programs across the University including the Bioinformatics and Computational Science graduate programs in the College of Science. More information is available at the Department of Mathematical Sciences' website: https://www.utep.edu/science/math/ . Job Description: The Department of Mathematical Sciences at The University of Texas at El Paso (UTEP) is seeking data scientists with expertise in Big Data Analytics/Machine Learning/Artificial Intelligence/Statistical Modeling and/or High-Performance Data Analytics for an open rank faculty position. Successful candidates are expected to develop productive research programs, mentor and teach at both the undergraduate and graduate levels and to direct Ph.D. dissertations in data science. Candidates with experience in industry and/or applied interdisciplinary research projects are encouraged to apply. Anticipated start date is fall 2027. Requirements: Applicants must have a Ph.D. in statistical/mathematical data science or a related field, a strong record of research accomplishments and the capacity to secure extramural funding. For recent PhDs, preference will be given to those with postdoctoral experience. Additional Information: Hiring decisions are subject to budget approval. In keeping with its access, excellence, and impact mission, The University of Texas at El Paso is committed to an open, diverse, and inclusive learning and working environment that honors the talents, respects the differences, and nurtures the growth and development of all. We seek to attract faculty and staff who share our commitment. The University of Texas at El Paso is an Equal Opportunity Employer. The University does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, religion, age, disability, genetic information, veteran status, or sexual orientation and gender in employment or the provision of services in accordance with state and federal law. Discrimination on the basis of sex includes an employee's or prospective employee's right to be free from sexual harassment under Title IX of the Higher Education Amendments of 1972. Inquiries-including the filing of a Formal Complaint or reporting an incident-about the application of Title IX may be referred to the Title IX Coordinator, who can be reached by phone at (915) 747-8358, by email at titleix@utep.edu , or by mail at 500 W. University Ave., El Paso, TX, Kelly Hall, Room 312. For accommodation information for employees and applicants with disabilities, please contact UTEP's Equal Opportunity Office at eoaa@utep.edu . To the extent that this position involves research, work, or access to critical infrastructure as referenced in Executive Order GA-48, being hired for and continuing to be employed in this position requires the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the infrastructure. Application Instructions: Review of applications will begin immediately and applications will be accepted until the position is filled. Candidates must submit: 1) letter of interest, 2) curriculum vitae, 3) statement of research interests, 4) brief outline of teaching philosophy, 5) copies of up to three relevant authored or co-authored publications, and 6) complete contact information for at least three references. Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs) Powered by Hide Main Navigation University of Texas at El Paso 500 West University Avenue | El Paso, TX 79968"
